THE UNIT, in full:

{
 "period_number": 3,
 "period_duration_minutes": 40,
 "progression_stage": 3,
 "stage_label": "Formulate an original scientific question and design a structured inquiry process",
 "synthesis": true,
 "roles": [
  {
   "actor": "Students (Individual)",
   "role_description": "Each student writes one original scientific question and a step-by-step plan for how they would investigate it, using the five-step structure as a scaffold."
  },
  {
   "actor": "Students (Groups)",
   "role_description": "Pairs or triads read each other's inquiry plans and give one specific piece of feedback on whether each of the five steps is genuinely present."
  },
  {
   "actor": "Teacher",
   "role_description": "Opens with a whole-chapter synthesis connecting the chapter's jigsaw metaphor to what students have done across all units, then circulates during writing and facilitates the closing discussion."
  }
 ],
 "activity_title": "The Mystery of the Wilting Plant: A Scientific Method Case File",
 "activity_description": "Students receive a set of 'Case File Cards' describing a household scenario — a potted plant that has begun wilting despite apparently normal care — presented in fragments across five cards, each card corresponding to one step of the scientific method. The cards are deliberately shuffled and some contain deliberate reasoning errors. Working first individually, then in small groups, students sequence the cards correctly, identify the flawed reasoning on the error cards, rewrite those sections, and finally compose a one-paragraph 'Case Closed' verdict that names what the scenario shows about science as a way of thinking. The task draws together every shared concept from the chapter — curiosity as the entry point, science as present in everyday life, the five steps of the scientific method, the idea that hypotheses are tested and revised, and the understanding that science is collaborative — by making those ideas do real work in a setting the chapter never visits.",
 "teacher_notes": "Prepare five Case File Cards per group before the lesson. Print or hand-write each card on a separate strip or quarter-sheet of paper. The five cards (described below) should be shuffled into a random order when handed out. Cards 1, 3 and 5 are correct; Cards 2 and 4 contain deliberate errors for students to find and fix.\n\nCard 1 — OBSERVATION (correct): 'Meera noticed that the plant on her windowsill, which she waters every two days, has drooping leaves and a dull colour. She finds this puzzling because she has been watering it regularly.'\n\nCard 2 — QUESTION (error): 'Meera asks: Why is everything in the world so difficult to understand? She decides plants are impossible to figure out.' [Error: the question is too vague and not connected to the specific observation; it cannot be investigated.]\n\nCard 3 — HYPOTHESIS (correct): 'Meera guesses that too much water might be drowning the roots and stopping them from working properly, causing the drooping.'\n\nCard 4 — TEST (error): 'Meera decides to believe her guess is true because she once heard that overwatering harms plants. She does not do anything further.' [Error: a hypothesis must be tested by actual observation or experiment, not accepted on hearsay without investigation.]\n\nCard 5 — ANALYSIS (correct): 'Meera checks the soil and finds it soggy even two days after watering. She reduces watering to once a week. After several days, the leaves straighten. Her hypothesis was supported; she also wonders whether the pot's drainage hole is blocked — a new question for a new inquiry.'\n\nDuring the group phase, circulate and listen for whether students can articulate WHY the error cards are wrong — not just that they feel wrong, but that they violate a specific step's purpose. Push groups beyond 'the question is bad' to 'the question cannot be turned into a hypothesis because it is not about an observable, specific thing.'\n\nThe 'Case Closed' paragraph is the synthesis product. It should name curiosity, a specific question, a hypothesis, a test, and the revision of understanding — the whole arc. If a group's paragraph omits any of these, prompt with: 'What step in the method does this part of the story show?'\n\nFor the closing discussion, draw out the chapter's jigsaw-puzzle idea: Card 5 ends with a new question, showing that answering one question opens another. This is the chapter's core metaphor made visible in the task itself.",
 "materials": [
  "Five Case File Cards per group — printed or hand-written by the teacher on paper strips or quarter-sheets before the lesson",
  "Blackboard or whiteboard and chalk or marker",
  "Notebook or loose paper for each student (for rewriting error cards and composing the 'Case Closed' paragraph)"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-6",
   "activity": "The teacher places a shuffled set of five Case File Cards face-down on each group's desk and says nothing about their order or content. Students are told only this: 'A girl named Meera had a problem. These five cards tell her story. Your job is to read them, put them in the right order, and be ready to say what Meera was doing.' Students read individually and write their proposed sequence in their notebooks. No group discussion yet — each student commits to an order alone."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "6-16",
   "activity": "Groups of three or four compare their individual sequences, agree on a final order, and then read each card critically: do all five cards show good scientific thinking, or do some contain mistakes? Groups identify which cards they believe are flawed and write, in their own words, what the error is and what the card should say instead. The teacher circulates, asking groups to explain their reasoning aloud rather than just marking a card as 'wrong.'"
  },
  {
   "minutes": "16-28",
   "activity": "Each group writes a 'Case Closed' paragraph — three to five sentences — that tells Meera's complete story in the correct sequence, using their corrected versions of the error cards. The paragraph must name each step of the method as it appears in the story. The teacher pauses one group mid-task and reads their draft aloud to the class as a shared model, inviting the class to check whether every step of the method is present."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "28-36",
   "activity": "Two groups read their 'Case Closed' paragraphs aloud. After each, the class listens for: (a) whether all five steps are present and in order; (b) whether the corrected error cards are genuinely fixed; and (c) whether the paragraph captures the idea that Meera's last line opens a new question. The teacher records on the board the five steps as groups name them, building the complete sequence visibly from the groups' own words."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "36-40",
   "activity": "The teacher closes by returning to what the task itself showed: Meera was not in a laboratory — she was at home with a houseplant, which is exactly where the chapter says science lives. Every step the class just used to fix and tell her story — noticing something puzzling, asking a focused question, guessing an explanation, testing it, and finding that the answer opens another question — is the chapter's method working in full. The board now holds those five steps, named by the class from real use, not from memorisation. The chapter's jigsaw-puzzle idea appears one last time: Card 5 ends with a new question, which means the puzzle has grown, not closed."
  }
 ],
 "homework": [],
 "pedagogical_approach": "Error-detection and reconstruction: students are positioned as critical readers who must identify where scientific reasoning breaks down, repair it, and then articulate what correct reasoning looks like. This makes the structure of the scientific method visible by showing what happens when it is violated, and it foregrounds the chapter's claim that science is a way of thinking rather than a set of facts."
}

