THE UNIT, in full:

{
 "period_number": 21,
 "period_duration_minutes": 40,
 "progression_stage": 6,
 "stage_label": "Apply classification and organism-environment reasoning to new contexts",
 "synthesis": true,
 "roles": [
  {
   "actor": "Students (Individual)",
   "role_description": "Work independently through all four parts of the synthesis scenario — classification, adaptation explanation, consequence prediction, and conservation proposal — producing a written response that draws on the full chapter."
  },
  {
   "actor": "Teacher",
   "role_description": "Circulates without providing answers, asking students to name the rule or criterion behind each claim they make, ensuring reasoning is visible not just conclusions."
  }
 ],
 "activity_title": "Reading a Marketplace: Classify, Connect, Conserve",
 "activity_description": "Students receive a set of illustrated market-stall cards showing nine plants and animals sold or traded at a rural weekly market — a wheat bundle, a rose plant, a coconut sapling, a pumpkin vine, a maize cob, a potted money plant (creeper), a live fish, a goat, and a pigeon in a cage. Each card shows the organism's visible features: stem type, leaf (with vein pattern sketched), movement organs if applicable, and one line describing where it is sourced from. Working in pairs, students classify every organism using the chapter's full framework, then use those classifications and the sourcing information to predict which two organisms would be most severely affected if one named habitat were damaged — and to justify that prediction by naming the specific feature that ties the organism to that habitat. The task is driven entirely by reading the cards, not by recall of chapter examples.",
 "teacher_notes": "The market setting is deliberately ordinary and local — it does not appear in the chapter, yet every concept the chapter built is necessary to work with it. Print or draw the nine cards before the period; each card needs only a simple sketch and three lines of text (feature list + source habitat). The sourcing habitat for each: wheat — river-plain farm; rose — garden nursery; coconut sapling — coastal forest edge; pumpkin vine — field boundary; maize — river-plain farm; money plant — shaded riverbank; fish — freshwater pond; goat — dry grassland; pigeon — open woodland near settlement. Assign one habitat-damage scenario per pair from the scenario slip: 'The freshwater pond has dried up', 'The coastal forest was cleared for construction', 'The dry grassland was converted to farmland', or 'The river-plain farm was flooded and abandoned'. Every scenario affects at least one organism on the cards and leaves others unaffected — this forces discriminating prediction. A strong pair will say: 'The fish is most affected because its aquatic habitat is gone, and its fins and streamlined body have no function on land; the goat is unaffected because its legs and dry-grassland source are untouched.' A weak pair will say 'the fish will die because it needs water' — push them to name the feature. The closing consolidation should not summarise the chapter's content; it should name the chapter's reasoning as the thing that made the task solvable.",
 "materials": [
  "Nine market-stall cards (one per organism, teacher-drawn or printed): each showing organism name, a simple sketch with stem or body, leaf venation sketch where applicable, movement organs labelled, and one sourcing line",
  "One habitat-damage scenario slip per pair (four scenario types, distributed across pairs)",
  "Classification grid worksheet (columns: organism name, plant/animal, stem type or movement organs, venation or locomotion type, root type if plant, habitat type, one adaptation feature)",
  "Notebooks or blank paper for the written prediction"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-5",
   "activity": "Teacher places the nine market-stall cards face-up on each pair's desk without explanation and says: 'A rural market sells all of these in a single morning. Each card tells you what the organism looks like and where it comes from. Your first job is to classify every one of them using everything this chapter gave you.' Teacher does not review chapter content — the cards are the entry point. Pairs begin reading immediately."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "5-18",
   "activity": "Pairs work through the classification grid, card by card. For each plant they determine: stem type (tree, shrub, herb, climber, or creeper), leaf venation (reticulate or parallel), root type (taproot or fibrous), and habitat type (terrestrial — specify — or aquatic). For each animal they determine: primary movement organs, locomotion type, and habitat type. Teacher circulates and asks pairs who are stuck on the pumpkin vine or money plant to re-read the stem description on the card — the question is whether the stem supports itself. For the pigeon, ask: what body parts does it use to move, and how many modes of movement does it have?"
  },
  {
   "minutes": "18-22",
   "activity": "Brief whole-class check on two contested organisms: teacher asks one pair to state their classification of the coconut sapling (tall, single thick woody trunk — tree; parallel venation on the sketched leaf — monocot, fibrous root, terrestrial coastal) and one pair to state their classification of the money plant (soft green stem needing support — climber; reticulate venation — dicot, taproot, terrestrial shaded moist). Class confirms or corrects using card evidence only. Teacher does not adjudicate by telling — asks 'what does the card say about the stem?' and lets the card settle the question."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "22-30",
   "activity": "Each pair receives their habitat-damage scenario slip. They must: (a) identify which organism or organisms on the nine cards are sourced from that habitat, (b) predict which one would be most severely affected, and (c) write two to three sentences naming the specific feature — from their classification grid — that makes that organism dependent on that habitat condition. They must also name one organism on the cards that would be unaffected, and state why its features and source habitat leave it insulated from this particular damage."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "30-37",
   "activity": "Four pairs — one for each scenario — share their prediction aloud. The class listens and evaluates: does the pair name a specific feature, or only a general dependence? For the 'freshwater pond dried up' pair, does the response mention fins and streamlined body or only 'fish need water'? Teacher keeps evaluation brief and peer-led: 'Does this prediction use the classification, or only the habitat?' After each sharing, one listening pair adds or challenges using their own grid."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "37-40",
   "activity": "Teacher closes by naming what the task demonstrated: classifying an organism by its observable features — venation, root, stem, movement organs — is not a separate skill from predicting where it can survive and what threatens it. The same features that placed the fish in an aquatic habitat, or the coconut sapling as a coastal monocot, are exactly the features that explained the vulnerability. The chapter's two threads — how to group living things and how living things fit their places — turned out to be one thread. The market was just the place where that thread became visible."
  }
 ],
 "homework": [],
 "pedagogical_approach": "Collaborative inquiry with evidence-based prediction: pairs jointly classify using observable features on the cards, then construct a spoken and written prediction grounded in the organism–habitat dependence the classification itself reveals."
}

