THE UNIT, in full:

{
 "period_number": 16,
 "period_duration_minutes": 40,
 "progression_stage": 5,
 "stage_label": "Analyse food systems and health connections",
 "synthesis": true,
 "roles": [
  {
   "actor": "Students (Groups)",
   "role_description": "Groups analyse the scenario, identify the nutritional problems, connect them to food system causes, and produce a written recommendation with evidence from the chapter's full content."
  },
  {
   "actor": "Teacher",
   "role_description": "Introduces the scenario, clarifies that all chapter content is available as evidence, and facilitates a final class-wide sharing of recommendations."
  }
 ],
 "activity_title": "The Hospital Canteen Problem",
 "activity_description": "Students receive a set of five 'patient profile cards' describing real-sounding but fictitious patients in a small district hospital canteen — a recovering child with anaemia in a jowar-growing district, a teenager athlete from a coastal fishing village, an elderly person in a Himalayan town with early signs of goitre, a young mother in a sugarcane-farming belt who relies heavily on processed snacks, and a construction worker in an urban area who skips meals. For each patient, groups must (a) identify which nutrients are most urgently needed and why, (b) choose three specific foods available in that patient's region that would address the deficiency or need, (c) run a quick paper analysis to confirm which of two unlabelled 'canteen food samples' (described on a data strip) contains starch, fat, or protein using the test logic from the chapter, and (d) flag any junk-food traps or high-food-miles risks on the proposed menu. A whole-class gallery of decisions is then interrogated: the teacher poses two cross-patient challenges that force students to articulate why the same nutrient can come from completely different regional foods, and why a balanced diet looks different in different contexts. The closing reflection names exactly what made the task solvable — the chapter's interlocking ideas — without rehearsing them as a list.",
 "teacher_notes": "Prepare five patient profile cards and two canteen food-sample data strips before the period — templates are described in the materials list and can be hand-written on index cards in about ten minutes. Each card gives the patient's age, location, physical activity level, current diet pattern, and one observable symptom or health need; the cards do NOT name the deficiency — students must infer it. The food-sample data strips describe two unlabelled items by their behaviour in tests (e.g., 'Item X: leaves a translucent patch on paper; turns blue-black with iodine solution') so that groups apply test logic rather than recall answers. Assign one patient card per group; if there are more than five groups, two groups may share a card and compare their recommendations at the end. During gallery interrogation, choose cross-patient challenges that force comparison: a good pair is the anaemic child (iron from green leafy vegetables, regional) versus the Himalayan elder (iodine from iodised salt, processed but necessary) — this surfaces the idea that 'local and minimally processed' is not a universal rule, and that protective nutrients sometimes require fortified or non-plant sources. The closing band should feel earned: name the ideas the class actually used during the task, not a list from the chapter contents page. Students who struggled to link symptom to deficiency, or who chose regionally implausible foods, will self-identify during the gallery; redirect gently by asking 'What grows there?' or 'Which test result tells you fat is present?' rather than correcting. No homework is assigned — the task itself is the synthesis.",
 "materials": [
  "Five patient profile cards (hand-written on index cards or half-sheets): each card states patient name, age, location and dominant local crop, current typical daily diet, physical activity, and one observable symptom or health need — symptom is described in plain language, not as a named deficiency",
  "Two canteen food-sample data strips (one strip per group, same two items for all groups): each strip describes how Item X and Item Y behaved in the iodine test, paper-patch test, and copper sulfate plus caustic soda test, giving colour or patch results without naming the food",
  "Blank decision sheet per group (four columns: Nutrient needed and why | Regional food that provides it | Test evidence for nutrient presence | Junk-food or high-food-miles risk flagged)",
  "Blackboard or large sheet of paper for the whole-class gallery (one row per patient group)",
  "Chalk or markers"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-5",
   "activity": "Teacher places a patient profile card and a food-sample data strip face-down on each group's desk. Without framing what the chapter covered, the teacher says: 'A small hospital canteen has asked us to advise on meals for five patients. Each group has one patient and two mystery food items the canteen already stocks. Your job is to work out what this patient needs, whether these items help, and what three regional foods you would add to the menu.' Cards are turned over. Teacher reads the two canteen food-sample test results aloud once so all groups hear the same data, then instructs groups to begin."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "5-22",
   "activity": "Groups work on their decision sheets. They first identify the nutrient gap from the symptom on the patient card, name the deficiency disease or health risk it signals, and select three specific foods available in the patient's region that address the gap — anchoring choices to the crops or food traditions of that location. They then interpret the food-sample data strip using the test logic: determine which of the two canteen items contains starch, fat, or protein, and decide whether each item belongs on this patient's tray or should be limited. Finally they flag any junk-food concern or high-food-miles risk in what they have proposed. Teacher circulates, asking probing questions: 'What symptom told you which nutrient was missing?' and 'Does that food actually grow in that region?' and 'Which test result are you reading to say fat is present?'"
  },
  {
   "minutes": "22-33",
   "activity": "Each group writes their patient's name and three key decisions (nutrient identified, regional food chosen, canteen item verdict) on the blackboard gallery row. Once all five rows are up, teacher poses two cross-patient challenges to the whole class: first, 'Two patients both need a mineral — the child in the jowar district and the elder in the Himalayan town. Their minerals are different and their sources are very different. Why does the solution look so different for each?' Second, 'One group flagged a food-miles risk in their recommendation. Another group's recommended food travels even further but they did not flag it — should they have?' Class responds briefly; teacher steers without resolving for them, pressing for reasoning tied to the chapter's ideas."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "33-40",
   "activity": "Teacher closes by naming, patient by patient, what the class actually did: 'To advise the anaemic child, you connected a symptom to a nutrient, found a regional source, and used a test result to evaluate a canteen item — that is the chapter working as a whole. To advise the Himalayan elder, you had to know that some protective nutrients require fortified sources because local soil lacks them — that is why the chapter has both regional food diversity and the iodine-salt story. Every patient forced you to use a different part of the chapter, and none of them let you use just one part.' Teacher then states the chapter's central insight in one sentence: food choices are mindful when they draw together what grows locally, what the body specifically needs, and how far food travels to reach the plate — and this class has just demonstrated that."
  }
 ],
 "homework": [],
 "pedagogical_approach": "Problem-based collaborative inquiry: students act as a nutrition advisory panel, applying the chapter's concepts — nutrient classification, deficiency-disease links, food testing logic, balanced diet criteria, regional crop-climate relationships, and food miles reasoning — to a novel institutional setting (a hospital canteen) that the chapter itself never visits. The vehicle is an error-detection and advisory-decision task, distinct from every final unit in the shorter plans, which used meal-plan design, evaluative argument construction, and justify-a-choice formats."
}

