THE UNIT, in full:

{
 "period_number": 14,
 "period_duration_minutes": 40,
 "progression_stage": 6,
 "stage_label": "Whole-chapter synthesis: describe and investigate magnet properties",
 "synthesis": true,
 "roles": [
  {
   "actor": "Teacher",
   "role_description": "Distributes the statement set, prompts students to identify which chapter evidence supports or refutes each statement, and facilitates a class discussion that draws all properties together."
  },
  {
   "actor": "Students (Individual)",
   "role_description": "Classify each statement, rewrite incorrect statements correctly, and identify which property or investigation result each correction relies on."
  },
  {
   "actor": "Students (Groups)",
   "role_description": "Compare individual corrections in small groups, resolve disagreements by citing evidence from any part of the chapter, and identify which property is most commonly misrepresented in the statement set."
  }
 ],
 "activity_title": "The Shipwreck Box: Reading a Crate of Found Objects",
 "activity_description": "Groups receive a set of 'shipwreck cards' — illustrated cards each depicting one object recovered from a fictional wrecked vessel. Using only the chapter's magnetic properties, groups must classify every object, identify any magnets among them, determine which end of each identified magnet is the North pole, and explain how the cargo's magnets could help surviving crew members find north without a map. Their reasoning is assembled into a short written 'harbour master's report' that uses each chapter concept exactly once as its justification.",
 "teacher_notes": "The shipwreck setting is entirely fresh — the chapter never uses it — but every demand the cards make is grounded in concepts present across all unit-title maps: the magnetic/non-magnetic distinction, poles as the strongest points, directional alignment, and the like-repels/unlike-attracts rule with repulsion as the definitive magnet test. Storage, damage rules, and the maze/garland applications are deliberately excluded because the shortest plan (8 units) does not cover them. The cards are printed double-sided: the front shows an illustration and a one-line description of the object; the back poses a single focused question about that object. Each group receives the same eight cards. Card 1 – iron nail (attracts to known magnet, does not repel: magnetic material, not a magnet). Card 2 – glass bottle (no response: non-magnetic). Card 3 – unmarked metal rod A (attracts AND one end repels a known pole: magnet — North pole identifiable by repulsion from the known North pole). Card 4 – unmarked metal rod B (attracts both poles of the known magnet, never repels: magnetic material, not a magnet). Card 5 – wooden plank fragment (no response: non-magnetic). Card 6 – unmarked metal rod C (attracts AND the opposite end repels: magnet — South pole identifiable). Card 7 – copper coin (no response: non-magnetic). Card 8 – iron ring (attracts, does not repel: magnetic material, not a magnet; poles are strongest at two points on the ring — mention pairing). The harbour master's report template (one per group, printed on a single sheet) has eight rows — one per object — and three columns: Classification, Evidence used, Chapter concept invoked. A ninth row asks: 'Using only the magnets in the crate, describe the simplest test that would let the crew find north.' This ninth row requires students to integrate the directional-alignment concept with everything they have concluded. The teacher's role during group work is to ask 'What would happen if you brought the known North pole close to that end?' whenever a group stalls on identifying a pole — a Socratic nudge that does not give the answer. In the closing band the teacher does not summarise the chapter from a list; instead they read aloud the ninth-row answers from two groups and draw out from the class why those answers work — tracing the reasoning back through pole identification, directional alignment, and the magnetic/non-magnetic classification in one connected arc.",
 "materials": [
  "Shipwreck cards — 8 cards per group, printed on card stock or heavy paper; front: simple line illustration of the object and a one-line description; back: one focused question about that object (teacher prepares one master set and photocopies)",
  "One known, labelled bar magnet per group (poles marked N and S)",
  "One magnetic compass per group",
  "Harbour master's report template — one A4 sheet per group (8-row table plus ninth row; columns: Object, Classification, Evidence used, Chapter concept invoked; ninth row: crew navigation question)",
  "Pencils or pens",
  "Whiteboard and markers for closing discussion"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-7",
   "activity": "Teacher places the eight shipwreck cards face-up on each group's table alongside a known labelled bar magnet and a compass, and reads aloud a two-sentence framing: a vessel has gone aground; a crate of mixed cargo has washed ashore; the harbour master needs a written report classifying every object and explaining how the crew can use anything magnetic in the crate to find their way. Teacher then directs groups to read all eight card fronts and, before touching any equipment, write a first-guess classification for every object in the Classification column. This prediction step surfaces prior reasoning and gives each student a stake in the outcome."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "7-28",
   "activity": "Groups work through the cards systematically, using the known magnet and compass to test each object. For each card they complete all three columns of its row: what the object is (magnetic material, non-magnetic material, or magnet), what they observed as evidence (attracted both poles / repelled one pole / no response), and the chapter concept that explains the classification (magnetic vs non-magnetic materials; poles are strongest at the ends; unlike poles attract, like poles repel; repulsion is the definitive test for a magnet; poles always exist in pairs). For cards 3 and 6, groups must identify which end is the North pole by testing repulsion against the known North pole. Teacher circulates, asking 'What would you expect to see if that end were a South pole?' to groups that have stalled."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "28-36",
   "activity": "Groups answer the ninth row: describe the simplest test using only the magnets found in the crate that would let the crew find north. Students must name which objects from their completed classification are usable, explain how a freely suspended magnet aligns with Earth's field, and state which end points north — connecting directional alignment back to the pole identification they have just done. The teacher reminds groups that their answer should be written so that someone who has not done the earlier rows can still follow the reasoning."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "36-40",
   "activity": "Teacher reads the ninth-row answer from two groups aloud without comment, then asks the class: 'What does this answer need from everything we did in rows one through eight?' Students name, in their own words, the chain: you need to know which objects are magnetic, which of those are actual magnets, which end of each magnet is North — and only then can you suspend one and use its alignment to find direction. Teacher closes by naming this chain as the chapter's central idea made visible: the properties of magnets — what they attract, where their force concentrates, how they align, and how poles interact — are not separate facts but a single connected account of magnetic behaviour that the shipwreck task just used whole."
  }
 ],
 "homework": [],
 "pedagogical_approach": "Collaborative evidence-based reasoning: groups treat a novel scenario as a complete application context, mobilising magnetic and non-magnetic classification, pole-strength evidence, directional alignment, and the attraction–repulsion distinction in a single coordinated written product rather than as separate exercises."
}

