THE UNIT, in full:

{
 "period_number": 14,
 "period_duration_minutes": 40,
 "progression_stage": 6,
 "stage_label": "Integrate measurement and motion: describe how position changes over time using standard units",
 "synthesis": true,
 "roles": [
  {
   "actor": "Students (Groups)",
   "role_description": "Work through the synthesis scenario, producing a written description that integrates all chapter threads: standard units and conversions, correct measurement technique, reference point, definition of motion, and classification by path."
  },
  {
   "actor": "Teacher",
   "role_description": "Facilitates without leading — asks groups to justify each decision by connecting it to a chapter concept, and ensures the final written description is coherent as a whole rather than a list of disconnected answers."
  }
 ],
 "activity_title": "The Harbour Scene: Reading a Place Through Measurement and Motion",
 "activity_description": "Students receive a detailed written description of a small harbour at a single moment in time — a scene the chapter never visits. The description names several objects and events: a fishing boat gliding away from the dock, a lighthouse beam sweeping in a circle, a buoy bobbing up and down on the water, a crane moving cargo in a straight line along a rail, and a fisherman sitting still in his boat. Students work first individually, then in pairs, to build a structured evidence table for the scene: for each object they state (a) its motion type and the path it traces, (b) the reference point relative to which that motion or rest is observed, (c) the most appropriate tool and technique for measuring any length or distance relevant to it, and (d) the unit in which that measurement would be recorded. The evidence table is not a summary exercise — students must apply measurement concepts and motion concepts together for each object, using the scene's details as raw material. Pairs then share one row of their table with the class, and the teacher assembles a class-wide version on the board. The unit closes by using the completed board table to name, concretely, the ideas that made the task possible.",
 "teacher_notes": "Read the harbour description aloud at the start — once slowly and expressively — before students see the printed copy. This gives the scene weight and ensures every student has the same information regardless of reading pace. The five objects are chosen to cover all three motion types (boat = linear, lighthouse beam = circular, buoy = oscillatory, crane = linear, fisherman = rest), reference-point relativity (the fisherman is at rest relative to the boat but in motion relative to the dock), and all three measurement tool choices (scale, metre tape, flexible tape or thread for a curved path). Watch for students who classify the fisherman as simply 'at rest' without naming a reference point — push them to specify 'at rest relative to the boat' and 'in motion relative to the harbour wall'. Similarly, watch for entries that name a measurement tool correctly but omit the unit. The board table should have four columns matching the four evidence items; as pairs contribute, stack identical answers and highlight any genuine disagreement for brief class discussion. The closing band should name the chapter's ideas by their proper terms, earned by pointing at the table the class just built — not by restating a summary from memory.",
 "materials": [
  "One printed harbour description card per student (teacher prints or writes the following on a half-sheet: 'At the harbour this morning: a fishing boat glides away from the dock in a straight line; the lighthouse beam sweeps round and round in a full circle; a buoy bobs up and down on the waves about a fixed spot; a crane slides a crate along a straight rail from one end of the dock to the other; a fisherman sits quietly in his seat inside the moving boat.')",
  "Blackboard and chalk (or whiteboard and marker)",
  "Notebook and pencil"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-5",
   "activity": "Teacher reads the harbour description aloud once, clearly and without interruption, while students listen. Printed cards are then distributed. Teacher says: 'This harbour is our workspace for today. Everything you need is in this description and in what you already know from this chapter. Your job is to read the scene like a scientist — precisely, with evidence for every claim.'"
  },
  {
   "minutes": "5-8",
   "activity": "Students copy a four-column table into their notebooks with headings: Object | Motion type and path (or: at rest) | Reference point | Measurement tool, technique, and unit. Teacher reads the five objects in the scene aloud once more and confirms that every student has listed all five as row labels before individual work begins."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "8-20",
   "activity": "Individual work. Students complete their evidence table for all five objects. Teacher circulates, asking individuals who have written a motion type to also describe the path in their own words, and asking individuals who have written a measurement tool to specify the unit and justify why a flexible tape or thread is needed for any curved path in the scene. No pair discussion yet."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "20-28",
   "activity": "Pairs form with the nearest neighbour. Each partner reads their entry for the fisherman row aloud. Pairs discuss: can the fisherman be both at rest and in motion at the same time? They must agree on a single joint answer that names two different reference points. Pairs then check all remaining rows together, flag any row where their entries differ, and prepare to contribute one row to the class table."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "28-37",
   "activity": "Class builds the board table. Teacher draws the four-column table on the board. Pairs are called on one at a time, each contributing one row; teacher writes their entry exactly as given, then invites a brief challenge or confirmation from the rest of the class before moving on. The fisherman row is taken last and discussed in full: the class agrees that reference point determines whether the same object is classified as in motion or at rest, and both answers are recorded side by side on the board."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "37-40",
   "activity": "Teacher points at the completed board table and closes the chapter by naming its ideas through what the table shows: 'Every entry in the motion-type column needed a path description — that is what makes a classification a justification, not just a label. Every entry in the reference-point column shows that motion and rest are not absolute facts about an object; they depend on the point from which we observe. Every entry in the measurement column shows that a measurement is only complete when it carries both a number and a unit, and that choosing the right tool depends on whether the path is straight or curved. These are the tools this chapter has given you. You just used all of them on a harbour you had never seen before.'"
  }
 ],
