 "activity_title": "The Cold Chain Problem — Keeping Vaccines Safe",
 "activity_description": "Students receive a data card describing a real-world logistics challenge: a rural health centre receives a batch of vaccines that must be stored between 2 °C and 8 °C. The card presents a partially filled temperature log — readings taken at different points in the journey (storage facility, transport van, health centre refrigerator) using different instruments — with some readings in °C, one in K, and one flagged as 'taken by hand, felt cool.' Students work in groups to audit the log: identifying which readings are reliable and why, converting the Kelvin reading into °C to check compliance, calculating whether the division value of the thermometer used in the van was fine enough to detect a violation, and marking any point where the cold chain may have been broken. The completed audit is then used to construct a short written protocol — three to four numbered rules — specifying which instrument, which technique, and which scale must be used at each stage to guarantee trustworthy records.",
 "teacher_notes": "Distribute one data card per group and let groups read it in silence before any class discussion — the scenario must land as a genuine problem, not a prompted exercise. The 'felt cool' entry in the log is the hook back to Unit 1 of any plan: expect some groups to dismiss it quickly; ask them to explain to the class exactly why it cannot count as a measurement. The Kelvin-to-Celsius conversion is the arithmetic crux; circulate early to catch groups who invert the formula (subtracting 273.15 instead of doing so from K). The division-value calculation is the same operation students performed on the laboratory thermometer: the data card gives two numbered marks and the count of divisions between them, so the method is identical even though the instrument is a refrigerator thermometer. Resist supplying the protocol rules yourself; let the audit findings drive them. A group whose protocol correctly addresses instrument choice, technique (immersed and vertical; eye level), scale, and the rejection of sensory judgment has demonstrated synthesis of the whole chapter. If time is short, the protocol can be condensed to two rules per group with a shared class list built on the board.",
 "materials": [
  "Data card (one per group, prepared by teacher before class — see content below)",
  "Blackboard or whiteboard",
  "Student notebooks or loose paper for the written protocol"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-7",
   "activity": "Teacher distributes the data card and reads the scenario aloud once: a rural health centre has just received vaccines; the cold chain log records temperatures at four checkpoints, but the log has problems. Groups read the card individually in silence, then the teacher asks: 'Before we do any calculation — which single entry on this log can you reject immediately, and why?' Students call out responses; teacher steers the class to the entry marked 'felt cool — no thermometer available,' establishing that sensory judgment is not a measurement and cannot substitute for one. This re-anchors the chapter's founding idea through the new scenario rather than through recap."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "7-22",
   "activity": "Groups work through the audit in three steps, recording findings in their notebooks. Step 1 — Reliability check: for each of the four log entries, students state whether the instrument and technique described (clinical thermometer at body-contact setting, refrigerator thermometer read through glass without immersion, a reading given as 279 K, and the sensory entry) is appropriate for the context and why. Step 2 — Conversion: students convert the Kelvin reading to °C using K = °C + 273.15 and determine whether it falls within the 2 °C–8 °C safe range. Step 3 — Division value: the data card states that the van's thermometer has numbered marks at 0 °C and 10 °C with 20 divisions between them; students calculate the division value and decide whether this instrument could reliably detect a reading of 8.5 °C (one that would indicate a breach). Teacher circulates, prompting groups who finish early to consider what a reading taken outside the liquid — through refrigerator glass — might introduce as a source of error."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "22-31",
   "activity": "Groups share their audit findings checkpoint by checkpoint. Teacher writes a running verdict column on the board: Reliable / Unreliable / Needs conversion / Instrument mismatch. The class negotiates any disagreements, with teacher asking groups to cite the specific chapter reasoning behind each verdict — not just the answer. The division-value finding (0.5 °C per division, so the thermometer cannot distinguish a reading of 8.5 °C from 8 °C or 9 °C) is discussed as a case where a technically functioning thermometer still fails the task because its precision is insufficient — extending the chapter's idea that reliable measurement requires the right instrument used correctly."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "31-38",
   "activity": "Each group writes a cold-chain temperature protocol: three to four numbered rules that a health worker must follow to produce a trustworthy log entry. Rules must specify the instrument type, how it is to be used (immersion, orientation, eye position), the scale and unit in which the reading is recorded, and why sensory checks alone are insufficient. Groups need not all produce identical protocols; the teacher invites one group to read theirs aloud, then asks the class: 'Is there a rule missing? Is there a rule here that the chapter gives us a reason to doubt?' This surfaces any remaining gaps without the teacher supplying a model answer."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "38-40",
   "activity": "Teacher closes by naming what the task itself showed: the scenario worked — the vaccines could be protected — only because every part of the chapter came into play together. Sensory unreliability explained why the felt-cool entry had to go. The definition of temperature as an objective property explained what the log entries actually represent. Instrument knowledge explained which thermometer belonged in a refrigerator context. Scale conversion let the Kelvin reading be checked. Division-value calculation revealed a precision limit. The protocol students wrote is the chapter's ideas functioning as a unified tool, not as a list of topics. No summary slide, no list of key terms — the audit and the protocol already did that work."
  }
 ],
 "homework": [],
 "pedagogical_approach": "Problem-based learning: students apply the chapter's concepts as a functioning toolkit inside a concrete, unfamiliar scenario rather than revisiting those concepts in the abstract. The temperature log is the site of synthesis — every concept the chapter developed (subjective sensation vs. objective measurement, thermometer types and their appropriate contexts, scale reading, division value, scale conversion, and the sources of measurement variation) is called into use by the audit task, and the protocol students write at the end constitutes their own articulation of why the chapter's ideas matter taken together."
}

