 "teacher_notes": "Distribute one copy of the laundry log per pair. The table has five rows; each row varies two or three conditions simultaneously, which is intentional — students must decide which factor is doing most of the work on each day and justify that decision using the chapter. This messiness mirrors real data and prevents mechanical one-factor-at-a-time pattern matching. In the pair phase, circulate and listen for students who invoke evaporation without connecting it to a specific factor (surface area, temperature, humidity, wind); prompt them with 'which condition on that day would have made it fast or slow, and how do you know?' The condensation question about the metal poles is embedded in the final prediction task; students who have only been thinking about evaporation must shift register and apply condensation reasoning — this cross-concept demand is the synthesis target. During the share-out, choose pairs who reached different conclusions about which single condition matters most on a given day; the productive disagreement surfaces that multiple factors interact. The closing consolidation should name evaporation, condensation, the factors governing evaporation rate, and the three states — not as a list to recite but as the reasons the table made sense once those ideas were in hand.",
