 "activity_description": "Students receive a set of situation cards describing a small flour-mill operator who has ended up with a single unwanted heap containing: wheat grains mixed with small pebbles, loose husk, iron bolt fragments (dropped during a repair), and a bag of spilled salt that dissolved in rainwater that soaked the heap. Students must read the heap as a set of property differences, decide which methods can be applied and in what order, justify each step by naming the physical property it exploits, identify where a physical change of state occurs, and produce a written separation plan. The plan is then tested against a class error-analysis card that describes a neighbour's attempted — but flawed — sequence, which the students must diagnose and correct. The task itself performs the synthesis: every concept from the chapter is called on by the properties of this single unfamiliar heap.",
 "teacher_notes": "This unit is the chapter's closing application. Every method introduced in the chapter is required by this heap: magnetic separation (iron bolts), winnowing (husk), handpicking or sieving (pebbles from grain), and evaporation (salt from the rainwater solution). The ordering question is the highest-demand move — students must realise that evaporation cannot come first because the water makes magnetic separation and winnowing impractical; magnetic separation and winnowing should precede any water-based step. The error-analysis card (prepared in advance) describes a neighbour who winnows first, then filters the wet salty mixture, then tries magnetic separation on the wet residue and fails — this gives students something concrete to diagnose rather than constructing a flaw from scratch. When students explain the error, press them to name the property that the wrong step requires that the wet iron fragments no longer display clearly in that context (the magnetic property is unchanged, but the practical difficulty is real — use this to discuss what 'method fails' actually means). The physical-change identification at the evaporation step is the second synthesis move: students should name it as a liquid-to-vapour conversion, connecting it to Activities 9.2 and 9.3 they studied earlier. If time is short, the error-analysis card can be discussed whole-class rather than in writing. No materials need preparation beyond the two cards described.",
 "materials": [
  "Situation card (one per pair) — a printed or handwritten description of the flour-mill heap and its four components with their observable properties",
  "Error-analysis card (one per pair) — a printed or handwritten account of the neighbour's flawed separation sequence",
  "Blank paper or notebooks for the written separation plan",
  "Pencils or pens",
  "One class property reference chart on the board (columns: component, key property, method that uses it) — drawn by teacher before the period if the class has not already built one"
