  },
  {
   "actor": "Students (Groups)",
   "role_description": "Classifies each resource in the scenario as renewable or non-renewable, evaluates available conservation options for each need, and produces a group written recommendation with a minimum of three chapter-grounded justifications."
  },
  {
   "actor": "Students (Individual)",
   "role_description": "After group discussion, each student writes a personal one-paragraph reflection on which single conservation choice in the recommendation is most justified and why."
  }
 ],
 "activity_title": "Designing Ramnagar's Resource Plan",
 "activity_description": "Students receive a one-page profile of a fictional inland village called Ramnagar and a set of twelve resource-decision cards. The village profile describes Ramnagar's geography, daily activities, and a list of six problems it currently faces — a drying well, heavy smoke from coal-fired brick kilns, a deforested hillside, eroding farm soil, frequent power cuts, and unequal water access between wealthier and poorer households. Each resource-decision card proposes one action the village council could take, drawn from resources the chapter covers but set entirely in this new fictional context. Working in groups, students build a written Resource Plan for Ramnagar: they select the actions they recommend, sequence them by urgency, classify every resource each action draws on as renewable or non-renewable, and write a brief justification explaining what harm each chosen action prevents or reverses. The plan is then presented to the class as a 'council report', and the class cross-examines each group's reasoning. The task is the synthesis — to build a coherent, justified plan the group must simultaneously apply the renewable/non-renewable classification, trace cause-and-effect chains between resource misuse and the village's problems, and weigh responsible use against present need, drawing every strand of the chapter into one working document.",
 "teacher_notes": "Print or hand-write the Ramnagar village profile and the twelve decision cards before class; both are described fully inside the time bands so no external source is needed. The twelve cards cover: (1) install rooftop solar panels on the school; (2) plant a community forest on the deforested hillside; (3) build a small stepwell to harvest monsoon rain; (4) switch the brick kilns from coal to natural gas; (5) introduce CNG three-wheelers for transport; (6) create a village compost pit using fallen leaves; (7) use wind energy to pump water from the existing well; (8) quarry the local granite ridge for new construction material; (9) ask residents to fix all household tap leaks; (10) organise a Van Mahotsav tree-planting drive; (11) install a solar water heater at the community centre; (12) restrict the number of trees felled each year. Groups must choose at least seven and may choose all twelve, but they must justify every inclusion and must explicitly address Ramnagar's six listed problems — each problem must be matched to at least one card. This forces completeness and prevents groups from choosing only comfortable options. Watch for two predictable gaps: (a) groups that classify natural gas as 'better than coal' without noting it is still non-renewable and finite — prompt them with 'does this solve the depletion problem or only the pollution problem?'; (b) groups that recommend quarrying granite without acknowledging the rock-formation timescale — the chapter supports responsible use of non-renewables, not zero use, so the correct move is to justify it while noting the need for restraint, not to ban it. The cross-examination phase is the most important cognitive moment; coach the audience to ask 'which of Ramnagar's six problems does this solve, and how?' rather than 'is this good or bad?' so reasoning stays grounded in evidence. No Gandhi quote, no Chipko reference, no Activity 11.6 list — the chapter's ideas are present through their operation on Ramnagar's problems, not through repetition of the chapter's own examples.",
 "materials": [
  "One hand-written or printed Ramnagar village profile per group (teacher prepares before class — content fully specified in the time bands)",
  "Twelve resource-decision cards per group, each card one sentence, written on slips of paper or card (content fully specified in the time bands)",
  "Student notebooks or loose sheets for writing the Resource Plan",
  "Blackboard and chalk for the teacher to track which problems each group has addressed during cross-examination",
  "Pencils or pens"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-8",
   "activity": "Teacher introduces Ramnagar by reading the village profile aloud while distributing one copy per group. Profile text (teacher writes this on slips or the board before class): 'Ramnagar is a village of about 400 families situated near a river, a granite ridge, and a hillside that was forested until ten years ago. Most families farm the land around the village. The village has: a well that is running low because the underground water is not being replenished fast enough; a row of coal-fired brick kilns whose smoke hangs over the village every morning; a hillside with almost no trees left; farm soil that washes away into the river after heavy rain; electricity that cuts out for several hours each day; and a water pipe that reaches the wealthier part of the village but not the poorer lanes.' Teacher reads the six problems aloud and writes them as a numbered list on the board. Teacher then distributes the twelve decision cards face-up and says: 'Your group is the Ramnagar village council. You have these twelve possible actions. Build a Resource Plan: choose at least seven actions, put them in order of urgency, and for each one write — which of Ramnagar's six problems it addresses, what resource it uses, whether that resource is renewable or non-renewable, and why you are recommending it.' Groups read all twelve cards silently before beginning."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "8-26",
   "activity": "Groups build their Resource Plans in notebooks. Teacher circulates, listening for reasoning quality rather than correct answers, and poses targeted prompts: to a group that has accepted natural gas (card 4) without comment — 'You've noted it is less polluting than coal; what about its replenishment time compared to solar?' To a group that has skipped the stepwell (card 3) — 'Ramnagar's well is running low; which of your chosen cards addresses that problem directly?' To a group that has left out granite quarrying (card 8) — 'The chapter describes rocks being used for construction; does responsible use mean never quarrying, or something else?' Teacher does not correct groups but keeps their reasoning moving toward precision. Groups must write at least one sentence of justification per chosen card before the presentation phase begins."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "26-36",
   "activity": "Each group nominates one spokesperson. Groups present their Resource Plans in rapid succession — approximately ninety seconds per group — stating only their top three urgency choices and their reasoning. After each presentation, the class cross-examines for ninety seconds: the teacher directs the audience to ask only questions of the form 'which of the six problems does this card solve, and through what resource mechanism?' Teacher marks on the board which of Ramnagar's six problems have been addressed across all groups, and which — if any — remain unaddressed. If a problem is still unaddressed after all presentations, teacher asks the whole class: 'Which card or cards would handle this, and why did groups leave it out?'"
  },
  {
   "minutes": "36-40",
   "activity": "Teacher draws the unit closed by naming what the task itself demonstrated: to build even one workable plan for Ramnagar required using the renewable/non-renewable classification to choose between options, tracing how each resource problem arose from a specific kind of misuse or overuse, recognising that non-renewable resources can be used responsibly rather than not at all, and seeing that the village's problems — smoke, soil erosion, water shortage, power cuts, unequal access — are not separate; they are linked because all of them trace back to how natural resources are managed. Teacher points to the board's list of Ramnagar's six problems and says: 'Every concept in this chapter was working inside your plan. That is what the chapter is for.' No further summary is given."
  }
 ],
 "homework": [],
 "pedagogical_approach": "Problem-based collaborative inquiry: a realistic design problem drives the integration of concepts. Groups cannot solve any single problem in Ramnagar without reaching across multiple resource types and the classification framework simultaneously, so the task structure enforces synthesis rather than topic-by-topic recall. Cross-examination by classmates adds an evaluative layer that requires groups to defend the causal logic of their choices, not merely list them."
}

