{
 "period_number": 7,
 "period_duration_minutes": 40,
 "progression_stage": 4,
 "stage_label": "Apply and evaluate — science as a collective human endeavour with values and limits",
 "synthesis": true,
 "roles": [
  {
   "actor": "Students (Individual)",
   "role_description": "Each student drafts a short written statement (three to four sentences) defending a position on what makes scientific knowledge capable of evolving over time."
  },
  {
   "actor": "Students (Groups)",
   "role_description": "Groups read each other's statements and pose one challenge question per statement; the author must respond using the chapter's framework of values and processes."
  },
  {
   "actor": "Teacher",
   "role_description": "Facilitates the challenge round and closes by articulating the chapter's core insight: science is not a set of facts but a collective, value-driven human endeavour that sustains its own revision."
  }
 ],
 "activity_title": "The Science Logbook of a Village Well",
 "activity_description": "Students are presented with a fictional but realistic science logbook kept over several decades by community members observing a village well — recording taste changes in the water, whether iron buckets rust faster in some seasons, what happens to the rope after years of use, how quickly ice forms on the stone rim in winter, and when the water level drops. The logbook contains good observations, muddled ones, one honest retraction, and several open questions no one has yet answered. Working with this single artefact, students practise asking testable questions, identifying what makes a question scientific, recognising scientific values in action, and tracing how partial knowledge builds toward fuller understanding — bringing the whole chapter to bear on a setting the chapter never touches.",
 "teacher_notes": "Print or write the logbook on a single A3 sheet or on the board as a sequence of dated entries — the entries are provided in the materials list. You do not need every entry to be perfect science; the mix of sharp observations, vague ones, one retraction, and unanswered questions is the pedagogical engine. In the first band, read two or three entries aloud and let the setting land before asking anything. The key moves to watch for: students who generate questions that are genuinely investigable versus ones that are too broad or unanswerable; students who identify the retraction as honesty rather than failure; students who see that the unanswered questions are not gaps to be embarrassed about but the forward edge of a living inquiry. In the final band, resist summarising the chapter's content — instead name the concepts (scientific questioning, evolving knowledge, collective endeavour, scientific values) as what made the task possible, letting the logbook work remain the evidence.",
 "materials": [
  "A printed or board-written 'Village Well Logbook' — a sequence of roughly twelve short dated entries spanning several decades, including: observations of water tasting more sour after rain; a note that the iron bucket rusted faster in the monsoon season; a sketch of ice on the stone rim with a question about why only the shaded side froze; a retraction entry reading 'I was wrong last year — the water did not smell of metal, I had been handling coins'; a note that a new observer has begun keeping records; an unanswered question asking whether the water level drop in summer is connected to less rain or to more people drawing water; and a final entry noting that the question is still open and inviting whoever reads this to continue",
  "One sheet of plain paper per group for recording questions and reasoning",
  "Pens or pencils"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-7",
   "activity": "The teacher introduces the logbook: 'This is not from your textbook. It is a record kept by ordinary people watching one well over many years. Let us read a few entries together.' The teacher reads three entries aloud — the sour-water observation, the rusting-bucket note, and the ice-on-the-rim entry with its embedded question — then asks the class: 'What kind of people kept this logbook? What were they doing?' Students respond briefly; the teacher does not evaluate yet, only accepts. The logbook is then distributed or displayed fully."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "7-18",
   "activity": "Groups each receive the same task sheet with three prompts: (1) Choose any two entries and write one testable question that each entry makes you want to investigate — a question you could actually try to answer, not just wonder about. (2) Find the entry where someone admitted they were wrong. What does that entry tell you about what science requires of its practitioners? (3) Find the unanswered question at the end. Is an unanswered question a problem or a feature of science? Explain your reasoning. Groups work through all three prompts, recording their responses on plain paper. The teacher circulates, asking 'How would you test that?' when a question is too vague, and 'What would count as evidence?' when a value claim stays abstract."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "18-29",
   "activity": "Whole-class share-out, structured by the teacher: first, two or three groups share their testable questions for one entry — the class votes by a show of hands on which is most investigable and briefly reasons why. Second, groups share their reading of the retraction entry; the teacher surfaces the word 'honesty' and asks what would have happened to the logbook's value over decades if that entry had never been written. Third, two groups share opposing positions on whether the unanswered question is a problem or a feature; the class discusses briefly, arriving at the idea that open questions are the forward edge of a living inquiry, not evidence of failure."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "29-37",
   "activity": "Each group takes one final minute to answer in writing: 'If a new observer finds this logbook a hundred years from now and adds their own entries, what makes the whole logbook — old entries and new — more valuable than any single entry alone?' Groups share one sentence each. The teacher collects the sentences without grading, using them only to gauge how explicitly students connect collective, cumulative effort to the quality of scientific knowledge."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "37-40",
   "activity": "The teacher closes by naming what made the logbook task possible — not a summary of its contents, but the concepts the class brought to it: 'You could find the testable questions because you know what a scientific question looks like. You could read the retraction as honesty because you know what values science depends on. You could see the unanswered question as an invitation rather than a failure because you understand that knowledge evolves. The well and its logbook are new — but none of that was new to you. That is what this chapter has been building.' No homework is assigned."
  }
 ],
 "homework": [],
 "pedagogical_approach": "Inquiry approach — students interrogate a novel primary-source artefact to surface and apply the chapter's core ideas, rather than being guided through those ideas directly. The logbook does the synthesising work: its texture forces students to distinguish careful from careless observation, to spot where values are present or absent, and to ask what question would come next."
}

