 "activity_title": "The Pickle Factory Problem: Reading a Substance Trail",
 "activity_description": "Students receive a data card describing a small pickle factory that produces waste water, uses cleaning agents on its equipment, and discharges runoff into a nearby stream. The card contains four observations recorded by a factory inspector — each observation is a colour change produced by a different indicator test on a different sample from the factory. Students work in pairs to read the colour-change trail, classify each sample, identify which samples are problematic, select a corrective substance for each problem, and write the neutralisation reaction for one correction of their choice. The task is completed on a structured response sheet. A whole-class share then names the chapter's ideas as the tools that made the reasoning possible.",
 "teacher_notes": "Prepare one data card per pair and one response sheet per student before the period. The data card (text provided in the materials list) contains exactly four inspector observations, each naming a sample and an indicator result — do not add explanatory glosses to the card itself. The observations are: (1) lemon brine from the pickling vat turns blue litmus paper red; (2) the equipment rinse solution leaves turmeric paper unchanged but turns red litmus paper blue; (3) the factory drainage water produces no colour change in either litmus paper; (4) the stream water downstream of the outlet turns red rose extract from red to green. Pairs will classify each sample, identify which samples would harm aquatic life if discharged unchanged (acidic or strongly basic), choose a corrective for each, and write one neutralisation equation. During pair work, circulate and listen for two common errors: treating 'turmeric paper unchanged' as evidence of acidity rather than recognising it could be neutral or acidic, and choosing a corrective without checking whether the product of the reaction would itself be harmful. The closing share should surface both of these reasoning moves. Keep your own closing remarks brief — let the pair-reasoning do the synthesis work, and name the chapter's central ideas only as the explanation for why the factory trail could be read at all.",
 "materials": [
  "Data card (one per pair) — teacher-prepared, printed or handwritten, containing the four inspector observations as listed in teacher notes",
  "Structured response sheet (one per student) — four rows labelled Sample 1–4, each with columns: indicator result given / nature of sample / would you treat this before release? / corrective substance if needed; plus one open box at the bottom labelled 'Write the neutralisation reaction for one correction'",
  "Blackboard and chalk"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-5",
   "activity": "Teacher distributes data cards face-down. Without distributing the response sheets yet, teacher reads the factory scenario aloud: a pickle factory produces four kinds of liquid — pickling brine, equipment rinse water, general drainage, and it discharges into a stream. A factory inspector tested each one with indicators and recorded only the colour changes. Teacher asks the class: 'What would you need to know, from indicator results alone, to decide whether any of these liquids is safe to release into the stream?' Take two or three spoken responses to orient the class to the task, then distribute response sheets and ask pairs to begin."
  },
