 "activity_title": "The Broken Bell — Diagnosing a Material Failure",
 "activity_description": "Students receive a brief written incident report describing a cracked temple bell that has also developed a greenish surface coating after years outdoors. A data card accompanies the report: the bell's fragments were tested and found to produce a ringing sound when struck, to flatten slightly rather than shatter under a hammer, to allow current through a tester circuit, and, when the bell's surface oxide was dissolved in water and tested with litmus, to turn red litmus blue. Students use this data, together with the chapter's full framework, to classify the bell material as a metal or non-metal, name the chemical process responsible for the green coating, explain what the oxide's litmus result reveals about the nature of metal oxides, and propose two practical methods by which the coating could be slowed or prevented. The task is structured as a written fault-analysis report completed individually, then cross-checked in pairs against a shared evidence checklist.",
 "teacher_notes": "Prepare the incident report and data card before the lesson — both are short and can be hand-written on the board or dictated if printing is not possible. The data card should list exactly four results: (1) ringing sound when struck, (2) flattens under a hammer blow, (3) bulb glows in tester circuit, (4) surface oxide dissolved in water turns red litmus blue. Do not name the metal; students will recognise the profile as metallic and the green coating as corrosion analogous to the verdigris on copper described in the chapter. The most productive error to watch for is a student who correctly classifies the bell as a metal but then proposes that the green oxide is acidic — the litmus result on the card directly contradicts this; use it to show how chemical data overrides assumption. A second error is listing rusting as the process; rusting is specific to iron and produces a brown deposit — the green coating on copper (verdigris) is the chapter's own example of a different corrosion process, and students should name corrosion rather than rusting. For the prevention step, accept any two methods the chapter names: painting, oiling, greasing, galvanisation — students should be asked to justify each choice in one sentence. The evidence checklist (five rows: sonority claim, malleability claim, conductivity claim, oxide nature claim, prevention claim) can be drawn by students themselves from their own reports during the pair cross-check, rather than pre-printed, which adds a metacognitive layer. If time allows, invite one pair to read their agreed report aloud and ask the class to judge whether each claim has its evidence.",
