 "activity_title": "The Steel Bridge and the Matchstick Forest: A Change Investigator's Casebook",
 "activity_description": "Students receive a two-page 'Casebook' describing a real infrastructure scenario — a steel railway bridge in a coastal region — and a controlled-burn forest management programme in the same region. Both settings are entirely new to the chapter, and both are rich with simultaneous physical and chemical changes, reversibility questions, desirability judgements, and slow natural processes. Working first individually and then as a pair, students fill in a structured case-analysis table: for each numbered change event in the casebook they must name the type of change, cite the observable evidence, state reversibility with reasoning, and give a desirability judgement with justification. The task ends with a short synthesis paragraph explaining why the bridge and the forest together required every classification tool the chapter built.",
 "teacher_notes": "Prepare the Casebook handout in advance (full text given in the materials note below). The handout describes eight numbered change events across the two settings; suggested events are: (1) steel bridge girders developing a reddish-brown crust after years of sea spray exposure; (2) bridge painters scraping loose surface scale away, changing the shape of the metal layer but not its identity; (3) a section of rock cliff beneath the bridge splitting along a crack after repeated wetting and drying cycles; (4) minerals in the cliff face reacting with dissolved salts in sea spray, producing a white powdery residue; (5) forest undergrowth being deliberately set alight — the dry leaves and twigs burning with flame and producing ash, carbon dioxide, and smoke; (6) larger living trees at the edge of the burn zone losing outer bark but surviving — bark chars and peels (shape/mass change), tree remains the same organism; (7) ash and charred material being washed by rain into a nearby stream, carried downstream, and settling as a fine layer on the stream bed; (8) the settled ash layer compacting slowly with sand over centuries into a new sedimentary layer. This spread gives every concept a home: rusting (chemical), scraping scale (physical), freeze-thaw crack (physical), mineral reaction with salt solution (chemical), combustion (chemical with fire triangle implicit), bark change (physical), sediment transport (physical-erosion), sediment lithification (physical, irreversible, slow). Desirability cuts across: the forest burn is deliberately desirable (land management) yet combustion is normally discussed as a hazard — this productive tension is worth drawing out. The synthesis paragraph is the unit's capstone; insist students name at least three chapter concepts by name in it. Common failure mode: students write 'it changed colour so it is chemical' without stating what new substance formed — press them during circulation to name the new substance or explain why one was formed. The pair consultation at minutes 18–28 is deliberately positioned after individual work so partners are comparing reasoning, not co-constructing from scratch.",
 "materials": [
  "Casebook handout (one per student, two sides of A4 — teacher prepares this before the lesson using the eight change events described in the teacher notes above, printed or hand-copied onto paper)",
  "Case-analysis table (printed on the reverse of the Casebook handout or on a separate sheet — five columns: Change Event | Type of Change | Observable Evidence | Reversible / Irreversible + Reason | Desirable / Undesirable + Reason)",
  "Student notebooks or loose lined paper (for the synthesis paragraph)",
  "Blackboard or whiteboard (for teacher's closing draw-together)",
  "Chalk or whiteboard markers"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
