 "activity_title": "Adolescence on the Ground: Tracing Biology Through a Community Health Fair",
 "activity_description": "Students receive a set of six information cards describing a fictional community health fair organised for adolescents. Each card presents a stall at the fair — its name, what it offers, and a brief profile of one adolescent visitor — without labelling the biology. Working in groups, students build a wall chart that maps each stall and visitor profile to the biological changes, hormonal drivers, and well-being measures covered in the chapter, then identify one stall that addresses more than one change at once and explain why that overlap exists. The completed charts reveal the chapter's full architecture — physical, reproductive, emotional, and well-being dimensions — held together by hormonal causation.",
 "teacher_notes": "The health fair setting is entirely new to the chapter; no scenario, character, or question in this unit repeats anything from earlier units. The six stall cards are designed so that each addresses a distinct domain from the chapter — voice and body structure, menstrual hygiene, iron-rich nutrition, emotional channelling through activity, hormonal explanation, and substance-free choices — while the final stall (Iron-Strong Station) visibly overlaps with the menstruation card, giving groups a concrete case in which to explain why the chapter's concepts are not isolated but linked through hormonal causation and rapid growth. Circulate during group work and listen for groups that fill in the 'hormonal driver' column with only the word 'hormones' — push them to be specific: which signal, which effect, which change. The wall chart vehicle is different in kind from the scenario-card analysis and individual written-response formats used in the shorter plans' final units; it produces a spatial, collective artefact rather than a linear argument. Prepare the stall cards in advance by writing or printing the six descriptions below on separate card-sized slips. One set per group is sufficient. If time is short, assign two stalls per group rather than all six and share results in the closing band.",
 "materials": [
  "Six stall cards per group (teacher-prepared slips, each ~5 cm × 10 cm, descriptions given in teacher notes above)",
  "One large sheet of chart paper per group",
  "Markers (at least two colours per group)",
  "A pre-drawn blank wall-chart template on the board showing four columns: Stall / Visitor Profile | Biological Change Involved | Hormonal Driver | Well-Being Measure Addressed",
  "Student notebooks (for individual jottings before group discussion)"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-7",
   "activity": "Teacher introduces the setting: 'Imagine your district has organised a one-day health fair specifically for adolescents aged 10–19. Six stalls have been set up, each addressing something happening in adolescent bodies or lives. Your group receives six cards — one per stall — each describing what the stall offers and one visitor who stopped there. Your job is not to design the fair; the fair already exists. Your job is to explain it — to show that you can read each stall and visitor profile and identify exactly which biological change, which hormonal driver, and which well-being measure from our chapter it connects to.' Teacher projects or writes the four-column wall-chart structure on the board, then distributes the stall cards face-down. Groups may not flip the cards until the signal. Teacher reads aloud Stall 1 as a worked example: 'Stall 1 — Voice Check Corner. Offers: a microphone and a simple pitch meter for fun. Visitor: Arjun, 13, notices his voice crackles when he speaks into the mic and he sees a slight bulge at his throat in the mirror next to the stall.' Teacher asks: 'What column entries would you write for Stall 1?' Takes two or three quick responses from the class to model the task, then signals groups to flip their cards."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "7-27",
   "activity": "Groups work through all six stall cards, filling in their wall chart. The stall cards read as follows — Stall 2: Cycle Calendar Stall, offers menstrual tracking charts and information on hygiene products; visitor Meena, 12, recently started her periods and wants to understand the cycle length and what products she can use. Stall 3: Iron-Strong Station, offers a display of iron-rich foods (spinach, kidney beans, dried fruits) and a quiz; visitor Priya, 14, often feels tired and the stall helper asks whether her periods have started recently. Stall 4: Move and Mood Zone, offers short group dance and sport sessions; visitor Rahul, 13, came because he has been feeling irritable at home and his friend suggested this stall. Stall 5: Why Is This Happening? Corner, offers a short talk with an illustrated chart of the brain-signal chain; visitor Divya, 11, wants to know why so many changes are happening to her body all at once. Stall 6: Choose Strong Stall, offers role-play cards where visitors practise refusing peer pressure to try tobacco or alcohol; visitor Karan, 15, says some friends have been pressuring him at school. Groups must complete all four columns for all six stalls; for Stall 3 they must additionally answer in a fifth row: 'Why does this stall overlap with Stall 2? Write one sentence using biology, not common sense.' Teacher circulates, prompting groups who write vague hormonal drivers to be specific about the chain from brain signal to effect."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "27-35",
   "activity": "Each group nominates one member to present the overlap answer — the connection between Iron-Strong Station and Cycle Calendar Stall — while the rest of the class listens and checks their own chart. After three groups share (in rapid succession, one sentence each), teacher opens a whole-class check on two columns most likely to be imprecise: 'Hormonal Driver' and 'Well-Being Measure Addressed'. For each, teacher invites a group to read their entry aloud, and the class gives a thumbs-up or a silent correction written in notebooks. Teacher draws attention to Stall 4 (Move and Mood Zone) as the case where emotional change and physical activity intersect — asking: 'Why does channelling emotion through physical activity appear at an adolescent health fair rather than a children's fair?' Elicits: rapid hormonal changes produce mood swings; physical activity is a constructive channel specific to this stage."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "35-40",
   "activity": "Teacher steps back from the wall charts and addresses the class directly: 'Look at your chart. You just explained an entire health fair using one chapter. Every stall made sense because the chapter gave you a connected explanation — not six separate facts, but one system: hormones drive physical, reproductive, and emotional changes; those changes create specific needs; well-being measures answer those needs with biological reasons. That is what this chapter taught. The fair worked because the biology works.' Teacher invites one student to name the single concept that runs through every row of the chart. Expected response: hormones, or the hormonal explanation. Teacher closes: 'Adolescence is not a collection of problems to manage — it is a stage with an explanation, and now you have it.'"
  }
