 "activity_description": "Groups receive a design brief describing a flat concrete rooftop in a hot city that is to be converted into a community garden. A set of scenario cards presents five specific problems: (1) metal water pipes on the roof are scalding to touch by afternoon; (2) a small glass greenhouse on the roof overheats inside; (3) the surrounding concrete pavement stays warm long after sunset while the garden soil cools quickly; (4) rainwater runs straight off the concrete roof edges and is lost; (5) workers sitting near a dark metal storage shed feel uncomfortably hot even without touching it. For each problem, groups identify which mode or modes of heat transfer are responsible, propose one material or structural change that uses a heat-transfer principle to solve it, and write a one-sentence justification. Groups then share one problem each, and the class constructs a shared explanation of how the chapter's concepts together account for every problem on the rooftop.",
 "teacher_notes": "Distribute one full set of five scenario cards to each group; groups do not divide the cards — all members read all five and discuss before writing. The order in which groups tackle the cards does not matter. During the share-out, assign one problem to one group but invite additions or corrections from the rest of the class before moving on. The most productive errors to watch for: (a) attributing the shed's warmth to convection rather than radiation — the worker is not touching the shed and there is no described air current, so radiation is the primary mode; press with 'is there any material between the shed and the worker that the heat must travel through?'; (b) explaining the greenhouse overheating without distinguishing that solar radiation enters through the glass but convection of hot air is trapped inside — both modes are at work, and the fix involves ventilation (convection) or a light-coloured surface (radiation reflection); (c) treating the soil-cooling observation as a conduction difference rather than a differential heat-capacity / radiation loss phenomenon — accept both conduction and radiation as valid contributions and reward reasoning that names both. The rainwater problem (card 4) targets infiltration and groundwater recharge rather than heat transfer directly; students who connect it to the water cycle driven by solar radiation are demonstrating the chapter's largest synthesis move. The closing band should name all five chapter threads — conduction, convection, radiation, differential heating of materials, infiltration and the water cycle — and point to the rooftop as the place where all of them appeared together.",
 "materials": [
