 "activity_title": "The Cycling Delivery Run: Time, Speed, and the Shape of a Journey",
 "activity_description": "Students receive a scenario card describing a cycle courier making deliveries across a town, accompanied by a partially completed distance-time table with deliberate gaps. Working in pairs, they fill the gaps using the speed formula and its rearrangements, classify each leg of the journey as uniform or non-uniform linear motion, calculate the courier's average speed for the whole run, and then construct an argument — using both numbers and motion-type labels — for whether the courier met a promised delivery deadline. The scenario is wholly new to the chapter; the concepts students bring to it (periodic timekeeping as the basis for reliable time data, speed calculation, the relationship between speed, distance and time, and the distinction between uniform and non-uniform linear motion) are the chapter's shared core, and the task makes those connections visible by requiring every one of them to answer a single practical question.",
 "teacher_notes": "Prepare one scenario card per pair. The table should have five legs; design the numbers so that two legs have constant per-interval speeds (uniform) and three do not (non-uniform), and so that the overall average speed lands just above or just below the deadline threshold — this makes the final argument genuinely contestable and prevents students from skipping the calculation. Recommended gap positions: leave the distance blank for one uniform leg (students use Speed × Time) and the time blank for one non-uniform leg (students use Distance ÷ Speed), so both rearrangements are required. During pair work, circulate and ask: 'How do you know this leg is uniform — what did you check?' and 'Could you say the courier was on time without quoting a number?' These questions push students toward evidence-backed claims. In the closing band, choose pairs with contrasting deadline verdicts first; the class resolving the disagreement by checking each other's arithmetic is itself a demonstration that measurement and calculation, not opinion, settle motion questions — the chapter's deepest lesson.",
 "materials": [
  "Scenario card (one per pair) — teacher-prepared; states the courier's name, the promised average speed needed to meet the deadline, and presents the five-leg distance-time table with three values left blank",
  "Blank answer sheet (one per pair) — ruled, with columns for leg number, distance, time, speed, and motion type, plus a box for the deadline argument",
  "Pencils and erasers",
  "Rulers (for neat table work)",
  "Calculators (one per pair, optional)"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-7",
   "activity": "Teacher places the scenario card face-down on each desk and asks the class to keep it unread for a moment. Teacher narrates briefly: a cycle courier in a town promises a customer an average speed of 12 km/h across the entire delivery run — if the courier's average speed falls below that, the delivery is officially late. Teacher then invites pairs to turn the card over, read the table, and spend two minutes identifying together: which values are missing, and which formula rearrangement will recover each one. Pairs report back aloud; teacher lists the two gap-types on the board (distance unknown → Distance = Speed × Time; time unknown → Time = Distance ÷ Speed) without solving them, establishing that every tool needed is already in the room."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "7-24",
   "activity": "Pairs fill the three gaps in the table, then for each of the five legs calculate the speed and record it, and finally label each leg as uniform linear motion or non-uniform linear motion with a one-sentence justification written in the motion-type column. Teacher circulates, asking pairs to articulate why equal distances in equal time intervals confirms uniform motion and why varying interval speeds signals non-uniform motion. Pairs who finish early are prompted to calculate the total distance and total time for the whole run before moving to the next band."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "24-34",
   "activity": "Each pair calculates the courier's average speed for the entire run and then writes a three-to-four sentence deadline argument on their answer sheet. The argument must: state the calculated average speed with its unit, compare it explicitly to the 12 km/h threshold, name at least one leg by its motion type as supporting evidence, and deliver a clear verdict — on time or late. Teacher prompts any pair writing only a verdict without a speed value: 'What number proves that?' Pairs swap sheets with a neighbouring pair for one minute of silent reading, then return them; readers mark with a tick any sentence that cites a specific numerical value."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "34-40",
   "activity": "Teacher invites two pairs with opposite verdicts to read their arguments aloud. The class identifies the source of any disagreement — arithmetic, rounding, or a missing rearrangement — and resolves it together. Teacher then closes by naming what made the task solvable: reliable, repeating time measurement gave trustworthy time data; the speed formula and its rearrangements let the class move between distance, time, and speed in any direction; and the uniform-versus-non-uniform distinction gave each leg a character beyond a single number. The courier's journey, seen whole, is the chapter drawn together — measurement of time and motion used as one connected tool."
  }
