 "activity_title": "The Diving Beetle's Day — Tracing Life Processes Through a Single Animal",
 "activity_description": "Students receive a data card describing a diving beetle: a small insect that lives in freshwater ponds, breathes air trapped under its wing covers, hunts small aquatic prey, and breaks food down inside its gut before nutrients are absorbed. Working from this single case, student pairs complete a structured evidence table that forces them to locate, in the beetle's biology, every major concept the chapter has built — mechanical and chemical breakdown, absorption, gas exchange, the respiration equation, and the dependence of cellular energy on both processes together. Pairs then use their completed table to write one joint causal paragraph explaining how the beetle stays alive through a single hour of activity.",
 "teacher_notes": "This unit works in both settings — as the chapter's true final lesson and as a fresh period after any shorter plan's own closing unit. In the second setting, students already have a diagram, a written explanation, or a failure-chain analysis from the day before; this unit asks them to apply the same concepts to a new animal without rehearsal. The diving beetle is chosen because every chapter concept maps onto it without any new biology: it breathes air (lungs would be misleading — insect tracheae are not in the chapter, so the card describes only the trapped-air bubble and gas exchange across its surface, which maps cleanly onto the chapter's gas-exchange mechanism), it has a gut that breaks food down in stages, and nutrients are absorbed into its blood and used in cells. The card deliberately omits any label or category — it describes features and lets students identify what each feature is doing. The evidence table has six rows, one per concept cluster; every row is drawn from the shortest plan's content (starch breakdown and chemical digestion, absorption in the gut, the alimentary canal as a continuous tube, the distinction between breathing and respiration, gas exchange driven by concentration difference, and the respiration equation connecting glucose and oxygen to energy). Do not add rows about peristalsis, bile, ruminant digestion, or the diaphragm model — those appear in some plans but not all. The causal paragraph is the unit's synthesis vehicle: it is not a list, not a diagram, not a scenario analysis, but a piece of connected prose that must contain at least two causal connectives ('so that', 'because', 'which means', 'therefore') linking digestion to respiration and both to cellular energy. Circulate during pair work and prompt any pair whose table rows are isolated by asking: 'Which row feeds into the next?' Highlight two or three paragraphs at the close that show the tightest causal chain — read them aloud and name the chapter ideas they used.",
 "materials": [
  "Diving beetle data card (one per pair — text prepared by teacher and printed or written on card: 'A diving beetle is about 3 cm long and lives in freshwater ponds. It traps a bubble of air beneath its hard wing covers before diving. While underwater, oxygen from the bubble passes into its body and carbon dioxide passes out into the bubble. It catches and swallows small water creatures. Inside its body, a long continuous tube breaks the food down in stages: first by grinding, then by digestive juices that split large food molecules into small ones. The small molecules pass through the lining of the gut into the blood and travel to every cell. Inside each cell, the small food molecules and oxygen react to release the energy the beetle uses to swim, hunt, and grow.')",
  "Evidence table worksheet (one per pair — six rows with headers: Concept from chapter | Where you see it in the beetle | What it does for the beetle)",
