 ],
 "activity_title": "The Floating Garden: Running a Plant's Life Processes on a Raft Farm",
 "activity_description": "Students encounter a brief description of a floating vegetable raft — a real agricultural system used in flooded or waterlogged regions, where plants grow on a buoyant mat of water hyacinth and compost, their roots hanging directly into the water below and their leaves fully exposed to the sky. Using only this new setting, student groups construct a data table that traces five named substances (carbon dioxide, oxygen, water, glucose, and mineral salts) through each of the three plant life processes studied in the chapter, recording for each substance: which process uses it, which process produces it, which tissue moves it, and the direction of movement within the plant. Groups then compare tables, resolve disagreements using the chapter's word equations and transport logic, and arrive at a shared account of why the raft farm — despite the absence of soil — can still sustain a productive crop.",
 "teacher_notes": "Draw the raft farm on the board at the start with a brief verbal description: a rectangle of matted water hyacinth floating on a river backwater, leafy vegetables growing upright on top, roots trailing into the water. This takes under two minutes and is all the scene-setting students need — do not lecture about the system. Distribute the blank data table immediately afterward so that producing it becomes the lesson's intellectual work rather than a receptive task.\n\nThe table has five rows (one substance each) and four columns: Process that uses it / Process that produces it / Tissue that transports it / Direction of movement. Some cells will be blank for some substances — for example, mineral salts are not produced by any plant process — and students must recognise that a blank is a correct answer, not a gap.\n\nThe raft-farm setting is chosen because it highlights xylem transport in a novel way: roots draw water and dissolved minerals directly from the surrounding water rather than from soil, so the question 'how do minerals reach the leaves without soil?' forces students to articulate what xylem actually does and why roots are sufficient for absorption. This is a productive point of confusion.\n\nMonitor for two common errors: (1) students placing oxygen only in the photosynthesis row and forgetting that respiration consumes it — the table structure exposes this immediately because the 'process that uses it' cell for oxygen will be left blank; (2) students writing that phloem moves water, or xylem moves glucose — the directional column makes this error visible when groups compare.\n\nThe closing band should not summarise the chapter verbally. Instead, ask one group to read their completed row for glucose aloud, then ask the class: 'What would happen to this raft crop if we covered every leaf with black plastic sheeting?' Let two or three students trace the consequence across all three processes before you name what the table has shown — that the three processes are one system, and the table is a map of that system.",
 "materials": [
  "Blackboard or whiteboard (for drawing the raft farm diagram)",
  "Chalk or whiteboard markers",
  "One blank data table per student (hand-drawn by teacher on board for students to copy, or pre-ruled on scrap paper — five rows labelled Carbon dioxide, Oxygen, Water, Glucose, Mineral salts; four columns labelled Process that uses it, Process that produces it, Tissue that transports it, Direction of movement)",
  "Student notebooks (for reference to word equations and transport diagrams from earlier units)"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-5",
   "activity": "Teacher draws a simple labelled diagram of the floating vegetable raft on the board — buoyant mat, leaves above, roots below in open water — and describes it in two or three sentences: plants grow without soil, roots hang in river water, leaves are fully in sunlight. Teacher asks: 'The farmer says this crop grows just as well as a field crop. Which three processes inside every one of these plants must be running for that claim to be true?' Students call out the three process names; teacher writes photosynthesis, transport, and respiration on the board. Teacher then draws and labels the data table on the board and instructs students to rule their own copy."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "5-18",
   "activity": "Students work in groups of three or four to fill in their data tables. Each student completes their own copy while discussing with the group. Teacher circulates, does not supply answers, but redirects with targeted questions: 'Your table says nothing uses oxygen — look at the respiration equation again.' and 'You have xylem moving glucose — which equation tells you what xylem actually carries?' Groups are expected to leave cells blank where a substance is genuinely not produced or not moved by any named tissue, and to justify blank cells when asked."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "18-30",
   "activity": "Two groups write their completed tables on the board side by side — teacher selects one group whose table has at least one disagreement with the other. Class examines the differences row by row. For each disagreement, the class identifies which word equation or transport principle resolves it. Teacher asks the group with the error to correct their board table and explain the correction in their own words. Attention is drawn to the mineral salts row, where the 'process that produces it' cell is blank, and to the oxygen row, where both photosynthesis and respiration appear in different columns — confirming that oxygen is both made and consumed, but not in the same process."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "30-37",
   "activity": "Teacher returns to the raft farm and poses a single consequence question: 'The farmer covers every leaf on the raft with black plastic for five days. Walk me through what happens to each process, in order, using your table.' Students write a brief individual response — three to five sentences — tracing the consequence from photosynthesis through glucose supply through respiration. Two students read their responses; class evaluates whether the account is complete and correctly ordered."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "37-40",
   "activity": "Teacher names what the table and the consequence question together showed: that photosynthesis, transport, and respiration are not three separate topics but one system in which each process depends on the others — and that the raft farm works, despite having no soil, because the plant's internal mechanisms (xylem drawing water and minerals from root to leaf, phloem carrying glucose from leaf to root, stomata exchanging gases, respiration running in every cell) are what make a plant a plant, regardless of what surrounds it. The chapter's central idea — that plants sustain themselves and others through these coordinated life processes — is what made the table fillable and the prediction possible."
  }
 ],
 "homework": [],
 "pedagogical_approach": "Collaborative inquiry: groups construct a structured data table as a thinking tool rather than a recording sheet, so that the act of filling it in forces negotiation about causal relationships between processes. Disagreements between groups at the comparison stage serve as diagnostic moments that the teacher uses to surface and correct residual confusions — particularly about the direction of transport and the continuous nature of respiration — before the whole-class closure."
}

