 "activity_title": "The Lighthouse Keeper's Problem: Light at Work Across an Island",
 "activity_description": "Students receive a set of illustrated scene cards depicting five situations a lighthouse keeper encounters on a small island — a fishing boat crew asking why they cannot see the lamp directly behind a rocky headland, a child whose shadow on the harbour wall keeps changing size as she walks, a foggy window that lets light in but makes the view blurry, a message written backwards on a tide-pool rock that only reads correctly in still water, and a tiny crack in a shuttered room that projects an inverted image of the sea onto the floor. For each scene the group must: name the optical principle at work, write a one- or two-sentence causal explanation in their own words, and draw a simple diagram tracing the path of light that accounts for what the keeper observes. In the final phase each group receives one additional blank card and invents a sixth scene on the island that would require a different principle from those already used — they swap cards with another group and solve each other's invented scenes.",
 "teacher_notes": "The five scenes are chosen so that every core concept shared across all plan lengths is called on: luminous versus non-luminous sources, rectilinear propagation of light, shadow formation and the effect of distance on shadow size, transparency or translucency of materials, the pinhole effect and the inversion of its image, and the laws of reflection including lateral inversion. Distribute the scene cards face-down and ask groups not to flip them until told — this creates a moment of shared anticipation and stops fast groups from racing ahead. Circulate during group work and listen for explanations that describe what happens without naming why: 'the shadow gets bigger when she walks closer' is incomplete; prompt with 'what property of light makes that happen?' to push toward the causal account. The blank-card invention step is the highest-demand move; if a group invents a scene that actually repeats a principle already used, ask them to read back through their five completed scenes and find a principle not yet covered before they swap. At the close, the consolidation should be earned by naming what the island task demonstrated: every one of the keeper's puzzles dissolved once the same principles — that light travels in straight lines and obeys fixed laws when it reflects — were brought to bear. Write the chapter's two load-bearing ideas on the board as the final act: RECTILINEAR PROPAGATION and LAWS OF REFLECTION, and let the island's five solved scenes stand as the evidence that those ideas are sufficient to account for the full range of light's behaviour covered in this chapter.",
 "materials": [
  "Scene cards — one set of five per group, each card bearing a short illustrated description of one of the five lighthouse-island situations (can be printed on plain paper and cut, or hand-drawn by the teacher on index cards before class)",
  "One blank card per group for the invented-scene step",
  "Plain paper or student notebooks for diagrams and written explanations",
  "Pencils and rulers for ray diagrams"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-5",
   "activity": "Teacher distributes the face-down scene-card sets without explanation and says: 'A lighthouse keeper on a small island has five things she cannot explain about light. Your job is to be the person who can explain them — using only what you already know.' Teacher reads the five scene headings aloud once so every group knows the full set, then asks groups to flip their cards and begin."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "5-25",
   "activity": "Groups work through their five scene cards in any order. For each scene they agree on the principle at work, write a causal explanation (why, not just what), and draw a simple ray diagram tracing the path of light. Teacher circulates, pressing for causal language wherever a group's explanation is purely descriptive, and checking that diagrams include straight-line rays and, where relevant, the normal and reflected ray."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "25-33",
   "activity": "Each group writes a sixth scene on their blank card — a new situation on the same island that calls on a principle not yet covered in their five completed scenes. Groups swap blank cards with a neighbouring group and solve the received scene using the same three-part format: principle, causal explanation, ray diagram."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "33-40",
   "activity": "Teacher calls on one group per scene to read their causal explanation aloud; the class signals agreement or offers a correction. Teacher then writes the chapter's two organising principles on the board — rectilinear propagation and the laws of reflection — and maps each of the five scenes onto one or both, showing that every puzzle the keeper faced was an instance of these principles. The task itself is named as the demonstration: the island scenes worked as a test because the principles are genuinely sufficient to explain all of them."
  }
 ],
 "homework": [],
 "pedagogical_approach": "Scenario-based collaborative reasoning: students work in small groups to analyse a set of narrative scenes rather than repeat a procedure, demanding that they retrieve, name, and apply the chapter's principles as tools for explanation. The invented-scene exchange sustains the generative demand to the end of the period and reveals whether students can produce novel contexts rather than merely recognise prepared ones."
}

