 "activity_title": "The Lighthouse Keeper's Log: Reading a Year of Sky Records",
 "activity_description": "Students receive a fabricated but internally consistent logbook kept by a lighthouse keeper at a location in the Northern Hemisphere. The log contains twelve monthly entries recording shadow length at noon, the constellation visible on the southern horizon at midnight, daily hours of sunlight, and one brief note about an unusual daytime or night-time sky event. No entry names a phenomenon by its scientific term. Students work in groups to annotate each entry with the correct explanation — identifying which motion of Earth (rotation, revolution, or orbital alignment) is responsible, what geometry produces the observation, and whether the unusual event is a solar eclipse, a lunar eclipse, or neither. Groups then construct a single annotated diagram showing the Earth's position in its orbit for each of the four key months represented in the log, and the class converges on a unified account of how the same two motions and one geometry rule explain every entry.",
 "teacher_notes": "Write the twelve log entries on the board or copy them onto paper before the period. The entries are designed so that: the June and December entries make the season contrast stark (long noon shadow in December, short in June; long daylight in June, short in December); the March and September entries show equal-length days; the constellation entries shift systematically, requiring students to invoke revolution and the changing night-sky direction; and two entries contain the unusual-event notes — one describing 'the Sun vanishing, two minutes long, at midday with a dark circle covering it, the sea birds going silent' (total solar eclipse) and one describing 'the full Moon turning a deep copper colour for nearly an hour' (total lunar eclipse, safe to observe). A third unusual-event note describes 'a tiny black dot crossing the face of the Sun over several hours' — this is a Transit of Venus, which the chapter's Solar Eclipse section addresses; students should conclude it is not an eclipse. The third unusual event tests whether students understand apparent size: Venus is larger than the Moon but so far away its apparent size is tiny, so it cannot block the Sun. Do not correct groups mid-task; let the diagram construction in the middle band surface disagreements, then use the closing band to resolve them. The most productive tension is usually between the shadow-length entries (which require rotation + tilt reasoning) and the constellation entries (which require revolution reasoning) — students sometimes conflate the two motions. The closing band's consolidation should name rotation, revolution, axial tilt, apparent size, and shadow geometry as the five tools that together explained every entry: this is the chapter drawn together by use.",
 "materials": [
  "Board or printed copies of the twelve lighthouse keeper log entries (written by teacher before class, following the content pattern described in teacher notes)",
  "Large plain paper or the back of used chart paper — one sheet per group — for the orbital diagram",
  "Pencils, pens, or markers",
  "Student notebooks"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-5",
   "activity": "Teacher reads the opening framing aloud: 'A lighthouse keeper on a rocky headland in the Northern Hemisphere kept a careful log for one year. Every month they recorded what they saw in the sky — but they had no science textbook, so they described everything in plain language. Your job is to be the scientist who reads the log and explains what was actually happening in each entry.' Teacher reads the first two entries aloud and asks the class: 'Which motion of Earth — rotation, revolution, or an alignment of Earth, Moon, and Sun — is this keeper describing?' One or two brief answers orient the class to the task without resolving it."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "5-22",
   "activity": "Groups of three to four students receive or copy all twelve entries. Each group works through every entry, writing a one-sentence annotation that names: (a) the responsible motion or geometry, (b) the causal link from that motion or geometry to the observation, and (c) for the three unusual-event entries, whether the event is a solar eclipse, a lunar eclipse, a Transit of Venus, or something else — with the reasoning based on apparent size and shadow direction. Teacher circulates, asking probing questions ('Why is the noon shadow shorter in June than in December — what has changed about the angle of sunlight?' or 'The constellation visible at midnight in March is different from the one in September — what has moved to change the direction Earth faces at night?') without supplying answers."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "22-33",
   "activity": "Each group uses their large sheet of paper to draw a single orbital diagram: a circle for Earth's orbit, the Sun at the centre, and Earth placed and labelled at four positions — the months showing the longest day, the shortest day, and the two equal-day months. On each Earth they draw the tilted axis and shade the illuminated hemisphere. They then add a small arrow or note at each position linking it to the log entries that belong to that month. Groups also mark, outside the orbital diagram, a small inset showing the Moon's position for each of the two eclipse entries, indicating the shadow geometry. One group member prepares to walk the class through their diagram."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "33-40",
   "activity": "Two groups briefly show their diagrams and the class identifies any disagreements — especially on the Transit of Venus entry and on which motion explains the shifting constellations. Teacher guides the class to a unified account: rotation explains the keeper's daily shadow arc and the apparent motion of stars each night; revolution explains the different constellations each month and the changing sunlight hours through the year; axial tilt explains why sunlight is intense and days are long in June but weak and short in December; apparent size explains why the Moon could block the Sun entirely while Venus could only cross it as a dot; and shadow geometry — who casts a shadow on whom — distinguishes a solar eclipse from a lunar eclipse. Teacher closes by naming these as the five ideas the chapter gave the class, and noting that together they are enough to read any sky record from any place on Earth."
  }
 ],
 "homework": [],
 "pedagogical_approach": "Collaborative annotation and diagram construction: students read a primary-source-style document, apply causal reasoning entry by entry, and synthesise their annotations into a spatial diagram — moving from text to geometry to unified explanation."
}

