 "activity_title": "The Cheese Dairy: Tracing the Chapter Through a Single Product",
 "activity_description": "Students receive a one-page profile of a small cheese dairy — its raw milk intake, the bacterial cultures it uses, the whey it discards into a compost pit, the algae growing on its roof-water tank, and a note that a virus recently caused a batch to fail — and work through a structured data table that asks them to identify, for each element of the dairy, the relevant cell type or organism, its cellular structure, and the chapter concept that explains its role. The completed table IS the synthesis: every row brings a different strand of the chapter to bear on the same concrete setting, and the pattern across all rows shows that the chapter's cellular and microbial ideas form a single, connected explanation.",
 "teacher_notes": "Write the dairy profile on the board or dictate it slowly before distributing blank table sheets — do not hand out a printed profile if printing is unavailable. The profile should contain exactly these six elements: (1) the cow whose milk is used — a multicellular animal; (2) Lactobacillus added to warm milk to set the cheese — bacteria, nucleoid, fermentation; (3) a small amount of yeast that drifted in from the air and made one batch slightly bubbly — unicellular fungus, cell wall without chloroplasts, fermentation; (4) the whey-and-peel compost pit outside — bacterial and fungal decomposers, cell wall, nutrient cycling; (5) a green film of microalgae on the water tank — photosynthesis, oxygen production, unicellular/multicellular; (6) a virus that destroyed one batch by entering the Lactobacillus cells — acellular, no cell membrane of its own, not independently living. Row 6 is the pivot: students must explain why the virus caused harm without being a living organism in the chapter's sense. The table has four columns: Element of the dairy | Organism or entity involved | Key structural feature (from the chapter) | Chapter concept that explains what it does. During the paired discussion band, listen for students who conflate 'has a cell wall' with 'is a plant cell' — fungi have cell walls but no chloroplasts, and row 3 tests exactly this. The closing band should not re-explain the table; instead ask two or three students to read one row each and then ask the class: 'Why did we need the whole chapter to explain one dairy?' — that question earns the closure.",
 "materials": [
  "Blackboard and chalk (for writing the dairy profile)",
  "Blank table sheets — one per student (four columns, six rows, ruled by hand or pre-drawn by teacher)",
  "Science notebooks for reference",
  "Pencils"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-8",
   "activity": "Teacher presents the cheese dairy scenario on the board, reading it aloud element by element and pausing after each to confirm students have noted it. Teacher distributes blank table sheets and reads the four column headings aloud: 'Element of the dairy', 'Organism or entity involved', 'Key structural feature', 'Chapter concept that explains what it does.' Teacher works through Row 1 — the cow — as a shared example with the class: multicellular animal, animal cells with cell membrane and nucleus but no cell wall or large vacuole, levels of organisation from cell to organism. This row establishes what a complete answer looks like."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "8-28",
   "activity": "Students complete Rows 2 through 6 individually in writing, working from their notebooks and the board. Row 2 (Lactobacillus): bacteria, nucleoid region not enclosed by nuclear membrane, fermentation converts lactose to lactic acid and sets the cheese. Row 3 (airborne yeast): unicellular fungus, has cell wall but no chloroplasts, fermentation releases carbon dioxide making the batch bubbly. Row 4 (compost pit): bacteria and fungi as decomposers, break down complex organic matter into simpler substances, nutrient return to soil. Row 5 (microalgae on tank): photosynthetic unicellular or multicellular microorganism, has chlorophyll, produces oxygen and is nutrient-rich. Row 6 (virus that destroyed a batch): acellular entity, no cell membrane of its own, can only multiply inside a living cell — therefore not a living organism by the chapter's cellular organisation criterion, but can disrupt living cells from within."
