 "activity_title": "The Fishing Boat's Power Problem",
 "activity_description": "Students receive a brief profile of a small fishing boat that carries three electrical systems powered by a rechargeable battery: a compass-deflecting sensor coil used to detect a metal anchor chain, a heating coil that warms the bait box, and the battery itself that must survive a full day at sea. Pairs of students work through a structured fault-finding log — a printed table with three columns (System, Symptom, Cause-and-Fix) — in which five entries are already partially filled with realistic faults. Each fault requires students to trace the chain from battery → current → effect and to name the chapter concept that explains both the failure and its remedy. In the final band, the class builds a single causal map on the board connecting every fault to the chapter ideas that resolved it, making the synthesis visible as a diagram rather than a list.",
 "teacher_notes": "The boat setting is entirely new to the chapter, but every mechanism it calls on — current producing a magnetic field in a coil, resistance converting electrical energy to heat, a rechargeable battery supplying and losing current over time — is taught in the chapter. Print one fault-finding log sheet per pair before the period; the five faults are listed in the materials. Fault 1 (coil stops deflecting compass when wire is disconnected) targets the magnetic effect and the on/off nature of electromagnets. Fault 2 (coil deflects more weakly after a sailor removes half the turns) targets strength variables. Fault 3 (heating coil no longer warms the bait box and the wire is found to be very thin and short) targets the factors that determine how much heat is produced — current, material, length — pulling in the idea that a shorter or thinner wire changes resistance and therefore heat. Fault 4 (the battery goes flat by midday instead of lasting all day) targets the rechargeable battery's limited charge-discharge cycle life and the fact that a dead battery is not harmless and must be disposed of correctly. Fault 5 (a sailor reverses the battery terminals to try to fix the flat battery and the coil's compass deflects in the opposite direction) targets current direction reversing the poles of the electromagnet. Pairs who finish early can be asked: 'Which fault would also apply to a dry cell, and which would not, and why?' — this stretches into the dry-cell versus rechargeable distinction. During the board map, draw a central node labelled CURRENT and have students place each fault's cause-concept as a branch; the visual result — all branches meeting current as the chapter's central agent — is the earned closure.",
 "materials": [
  "Printed fault-finding log sheet (one per pair) — five rows, three columns: System | Symptom | Cause-and-Fix; rows 1–5 have System and Symptom pre-filled, Cause-and-Fix blank",
