  "Chalk or whiteboard markers",
  "Pencils or pens"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-8",
   "activity": "Teacher reads aloud the boat profile: a small fishing vessel runs three electrical systems from a single rechargeable battery — a sensor coil near the anchor-chain pulley (a coil of wire around an iron core connected to a compass needle indicator), a bait-box heating coil, and the battery that must last a full working day. Teacher distributes the fault-finding log and asks pairs to read all five faults silently, then identify for each fault which of the chapter's three topics — magnetic effect, heating effect, or the battery and cell — is involved. Pairs record their initial classification in pencil beside each row. No answers yet; this classification primes the chapter's structure before reasoning begins."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "8-30",
   "activity": "Pairs work through the Cause-and-Fix column for each fault in order. Fault 1: the compass indicator stops deflecting when the wire to the coil works loose — pairs must explain that the magnetic field around the coil disappears when current stops, and the fix is to restore the circuit so current flows again. Fault 2: a sailor removes half the turns from the sensor coil to shorten it and the compass deflection becomes noticeably weaker — pairs must name the number of coil turns as a factor controlling electromagnet strength, and state that restoring the turns will restore the field. Fault 3: the heating coil in the bait box no longer warms adequately after a replacement coil was installed; the replacement is shorter and thinner than the original — pairs must connect wire length and thickness to the amount of heat produced, explaining that the resistance and therefore the heat generated depends on these properties of the wire. Fault 4: by midday the battery can no longer drive either effect; the crew find the battery is several years old and has been charged and discharged daily — pairs must explain that rechargeable batteries degrade after many charge-discharge cycles and eventually cannot hold enough charge to drive current; they must also note that the old battery should not be thrown overboard but taken to a proper disposal facility because it may contain acids and toxic materials. Fault 5: trying to revive the battery, a sailor connects it the wrong way round; the anchor-chain sensor's compass deflects, but in the opposite direction from normal — pairs must explain that reversing current direction reverses the poles of the electromagnet, so the compass deflects the other way. Teacher circulates, asking pairs who have written a correct cause to state the remedy in their own words before moving on, and asking pairs who are stuck on Fault 3 to recall what they found when they compared one cell with two cells in the heating activity."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "30-42",
   "activity": "Whole-class board synthesis. Teacher draws a single central node labelled CURRENT on the board. For each fault in turn, one pair reads their Cause-and-Fix aloud. Teacher writes the concept name as a branch from CURRENT — for example: 'circuit must be closed → magnetic field exists only when current flows'; 'number of turns → controls electromagnet strength'; 'wire length and thickness → controls heat produced'; 'charge-discharge cycles exhaust the battery → less current available'; 'current direction → determines pole orientation'. After all five branches are on the board, teacher asks the class: 'What is sitting at the centre of every single fix?' Students identify current as the agent that links every fault. Teacher then asks: 'What supplied the current for all of these systems?' — drawing out the rechargeable battery as the source. The map on the board now shows source → current → branches to magnetic effect concepts and heating effect concepts, with the battery's limits as the reason all effects can fail."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "42-45",
   "activity": "Teacher closes by pointing to the finished board map and naming what it shows: the chapter is one idea in three expressions — a source drives a current, the current produces a magnetic field wherever it flows through a coil, and it produces heat wherever it meets resistance, and the source itself has a chemistry and a lifespan that set the limit on everything else. The boat's faults worked because the chapter's concepts were all that was needed to diagnose and fix them. That is why the chapter matters: not as a list of effects to memorise, but as a set of connected causal chains that explain — and fix — the world."
  }
