 "activity_title": "The Harbour Engineer's Problem",
 "activity_description": "Students take on the role of harbour engineers examining a data log from a small coastal harbour. The log describes five real events that occurred on a single day — a laden cargo barge sitting lower in the water than expected, a ferry magnetised by its steel hull attracting loose nuts during docking, a dockworker's trolley rolling farther than usual after being pushed across a wet concrete apron, a buoy ripped from its anchor chain floating freely, and a crane hook left hanging that stretched its calibrated spring scale by a measurable amount. For each event, students construct a written force account: they name every force acting, classify each as contact or non-contact, identify the two interacting objects, state the effect the force produces (start motion, stop motion, change direction, change shape, or sustain a force balance), and — for the barge and crane events — apply the weight and buoyancy frameworks to explain the numerical outcome. The five accounts are then assembled into a single annotated harbour diagram, making visible, in one artefact, every major concept the chapter contains.",
 "teacher_notes": "Open directly with the harbour log — do not frame this as a review. The novelty of the setting is deliberate; students must recognise which tools apply, not be told. Every concept tested here appears in all four shorter plans and across the fullest plan: force as a push or pull between two objects; the four effects of force; contact versus non-contact classification; muscular force, friction, magnetic force, electrostatic force (implicitly, via magnetism), gravitational force; weight in newtons; buoyant force and the gravity-upthrust balance; and the role of surface character in friction. Do not use the electrostatic force from Section 5.4.2 as a separate event — the magnetised-hull event covers the non-contact, at-a-distance strand; electrostatic force is not in the shared concept set of all shorter plans and so is correctly excluded. During pair work, listen for students who name gravitational force for the barge but forget that buoyant force must also be named and compared — this is the most common incomplete account. For the trolley event, prompt pairs who write only 'friction' to also name the surface interaction and state friction's direction relative to motion. The annotated diagram does not need to be artistically elaborate; arrows labelled with force name, type, and direction are sufficient. Collect the written accounts at the end as a formative record; the diagram can remain on the desk for a brief share-out.",
 "materials": [
  "One printed or board-displayed harbour data log per pair (five events described in plain prose, as given in the activity description)",
  "One large blank sheet of paper per pair for the annotated harbour diagram",
  "Pencils and, if available, two colours of pen (one for contact forces, one for non-contact forces)",
  "Notebooks or lined paper for the individual written force accounts"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-7",
   "activity": "Teacher places the harbour data log in front of every pair and reads Event 1 aloud — the laden cargo barge sitting lower in the water than its normal load line. Teacher asks: 'How many forces are acting on this barge right now, and are they in balance or out of balance?' Pairs talk for ninety seconds; two or three responses are heard. Teacher does not confirm or correct — notes the range of answers on the board and says: 'By the time you have written your five force accounts, you will be able to answer that for every event.' No further framing is given; pairs begin work."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "7-28",
   "activity": "Pairs work through all five harbour events in sequence, writing a force account for each in their notebooks. The account must contain: (a) the names of the two interacting objects, (b) the force name and contact or non-contact classification, (c) the direction of the force on each object, (d) the effect produced from the chapter's list of four effects or, where appropriate, a statement of force balance, and (e) for Events 1 and 5 (the barge and the crane hook), a comparison of gravitational force and buoyant force or the identification of what the spring scale reading represents in newtons. Teacher circulates without supplying answers, asking probing questions: 'What is the second object in the magnetic interaction?' 'Which direction does friction act on the trolley — same as motion or opposite?' 'If the barge is lower than expected, is upthrust greater than gravity, equal, or less?' Pairs who finish early are asked to re-examine their account for Event 3 (the wet concrete apron) and explain why the surface condition changes the outcome."
