 "activity_description": "Students receive a brief design brief about a food-technology problem — a factory wants to ship flavoured ice lollies to a hot region without a cold chain — and work in pairs to produce an annotated design response. The design task requires them to apply every shared concept of the chapter: constituent particles and interparticle spaces, the three states and their observable properties, interparticle attraction and spacing, compressibility, particle motion, thermal energy as a driver, and the melting and boiling points as thresholds. Each pair produces a single annotated diagram and a short written rationale. The class finishes by comparing two contrasting designs and naming the chapter's central ideas as the reason the task could be solved at all.",
 "teacher_notes": "The ice-lolly scenario is deliberately chosen because it is food-familiar and state-change-rich without appearing in any of the shorter plan final units. The design task is open enough that pairs will make different choices — some will focus on insulation slowing heat transfer, others on choosing a substance with a higher melting point, others on packaging that traps gas — and all of these are valid starting points, but every choice must be justified with particle-level reasoning. Circulate during pair work with a single probe question held in reserve: 'What is happening to the interparticle attractions as the lolly warms up?' This steers any pair that has slipped into everyday language back to the model. For the comparison in the final band, choose two designs that differ in which part of the model they emphasise — one that foregrounds thermal energy and melting point, one that foregrounds interparticle spacing and compressibility — so the class discussion naturally covers the whole chapter. In the closing, avoid asking students to 'summarise the chapter'; instead ask 'why was the particulate model useful here?' — the answer names the chapter's ideas as tools, which is earned closure rather than ceremony.",
 "materials": [
  "Design brief card (one per pair, printed or handwritten on card): 'A factory makes flavoured ice lollies. They need to transport them for 6 hours to a shop in a hot region with no refrigeration. Design a simple packaging solution and explain, using the particle model, why your design slows down or prevents melting.'",
  "Plain A4 paper (two sheets per pair, one for the annotated diagram, one for the written rationale)",
  "Pencils and rulers (one set per pair)",
  "Coloured markers or crayons for particle-level diagrams (one set per pair)",
  "Class whiteboard or chalkboard for displaying two chosen designs at the end"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-7",
   "activity": "Teacher places the design brief card on each pair's desk and reads it aloud. Without explaining how to solve it, the teacher asks the class one orienting question: 'An ice lolly is a solid. What has to happen at the particle level for it to become a liquid?' A few students respond — teacher accepts answers, writes three key terms on the board (thermal energy, interparticle attraction, melting point) and says: 'Your design has to slow or stop exactly that process. You decide how. Use the card, use those terms, and use everything else from the chapter you need.'"
  },
  {
   "minutes": "7-30",
   "activity": "Pairs work simultaneously on the annotated diagram and the written rationale. The diagram must show particle-level sketches of the lolly in its solid state and what happens as thermal energy increases; the rationale must explain, in prose, how their packaging choice interferes with that process. Teacher circulates, listening for pairs reasoning only at the everyday level ('we wrap it in foil to keep it cold') and redirecting them to the particle level ('tell me what the foil does to the rate at which thermal energy reaches the interparticle attractions inside the lolly'). Teacher also listens for pairs who invoke compressibility, interparticle spacing, or particle motion in gases (e.g., trapped air as insulation) and encourages them to make that reasoning explicit in their written rationale."
