 "activity_description": "Students receive a data card describing a river water sample collected downstream from a town. The card lists observations: the water looks slightly cloudy, smells faintly of rotten eggs, leaves a white crust on a glass dish when evaporated, turns lime water milky when air bubbled through the residue is tested, and a small piece of iron left in it for two days turns reddish-brown. A dissolved-gas reading shows two gases present in unequal proportions. Students use these observations as evidence to identify what the river water contains — distinguishing the water itself, dissolved gases, suspended solids, and a dissolved compound — and to classify each identified component as element, compound, or mixture, building a labelled classification map. The map is then used to argue whether the river water as a whole is a mixture, and why its components behave as they do.",
 "teacher_notes": "The river water setting is entirely fresh — the chapter uses seawater and aerated water only as listed examples in a table, never as an investigative focus. Every concept the task calls on (mixture vs. pure substance, uniform vs. non-uniform mixture, element vs. compound, fixed-ratio properties of compounds, properties retained by mixture components) appears in all four unit-title maps. Draw students' attention to the unequal gas proportions clue: this mirrors the electrolysis observation from their earlier work and should prompt them to distinguish dissolved oxygen (an element) from dissolved carbon dioxide (a compound). The white crust is calcium carbonate — a compound — and the reddish-brown iron is iron oxide (rust), another compound formed because the river water is a mixture that allows its components to interact with things placed in it, not with each other chemically. The rotten-egg smell is the most demanding clue: it points to dissolved hydrogen sulfide, a compound. Do not confirm identities in advance; let groups commit to their labels and defend them. If a group classifies the river water itself as a pure substance, ask them to apply the single-kind-of-particle test. The closing consolidation should name the three ideas that made the task workable — the mixture definition, the element/compound distinction via properties and breakability, and the fixed-ratio criterion — as the reason the river's complexity became readable.",
