  {
   "minutes": "0-8",
   "activity": "Teacher distributes the Coral Reef Field Report sheet and reads the opening paragraph aloud while students follow: 'A research team recorded conditions at two depths in a warm, shallow reef — the surface layer (30 °C) and the bottom layer (18 °C). At the surface they measured higher salinity: 38 g of salt dissolved per litre of water. At the bottom salinity was 34 g per litre. The team also found that fish and sea creatures were far more numerous at the bottom than the surface. They collected three objects from the reef floor: Object A (mass 48 g, volume 40 cm³), Object B (mass 27 g, volume 36 cm³), and Object C (mass 55 g, volume 50 cm³). The water density at the bottom layer is 1.026 g/cm³.' Teacher then poses the opening question to the whole class without accepting answers yet: 'Which layer holds more dissolved oxygen, and why? Which objects would you expect to find resting on the floor rather than floating up?' Students read the full report silently and begin the annotation sheet individually."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "8-18",
   "activity": "Students work individually on the first half of the annotation sheet: the three rows covering the solution strand — (1) classifying both water layers as solutions and identifying solute and solvent, (2) comparing the concentration of the two layers using the salinity figures and stating which is more concentrated, and (3) explaining the dissolved-oxygen contrast between the warm surface and the cool bottom using the gas-solubility rule. Teacher circulates, checking that the 'Concept applied' column names a specific term from the chapter (not just 'solutions') and that the reasoning column uses the numerical salinity data, not just a general statement."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "18-32",
   "activity": "Students move to the density strand rows — (4) calculating the density of each of the three objects using mass ÷ volume and recording units, (5) comparing each object's density to the bottom-layer water density (1.026 g/cm³) and predicting float or sink with a relative-density statement, (6) explaining in one sentence why the surface layer water is denser than pure water and how the dissolved salt contributes to that. Groups of three are formed at this point; students compare their calculations for Objects A, B, and C, resolve any discrepancies, and agree on a single set of values. Teacher moves between groups and asks: 'What does your relative density value tell you? State it as a comparison to 1.' A brief whole-class check is called at minute 28: teacher asks one group to call out the three density values; class confirms or corrects. (Object A: 1.2 g/cm³ — sinks; Object B: 0.75 g/cm³ — floats; Object C: 1.1 g/cm³ — sinks.)"
  },
  {
   "minutes": "32-40",
   "activity": "Groups tackle the final two rows of the annotation sheet, which require both strands together: (7) explaining why fish concentrate at the bottom layer by linking dissolved-oxygen availability (gas solubility decreasing with temperature) to the conditions at 18 °C versus 30 °C, and (8) predicting what would happen to Object B if the temperature of the water dropped significantly — whether the water's density would change, whether that affects the float-or-sink outcome, and what the particle-level reason is. Each group writes a two-sentence combined answer for row 8 that names one concept from each strand. Teacher listens in and selects two groups with different phrasings of row 8 to share in the closing band."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "40-45",
   "activity": "The two selected groups read their row-8 annotation aloud. Class evaluates: does each sentence name a distinct concept, and do the two sentences connect? Teacher then closes not by summarising the chapter but by naming what the annotation sheet demonstrated: every entry required either identifying what kind of mixture the water is and how much it can hold, or measuring and comparing how much matter occupies a given space — and the reef made both questions necessary at the same time. The chapter's work — defining solutions and measuring density — turned a pile of field data into explanations."
  }
 ],
