{
 "period_number": 15,
 "period_duration_minutes": 45,
 "progression_stage": 4,
 "stage_label": "Apply the periodic-motion framework to satellites and space observation",
 "synthesis": true,
 "roles": [
  {
   "actor": "Teacher",
   "role_description": "Distributes the synthesis prompt cards and poses the unifying question: what single physical principle connects Moon phases, calendar structure, festival timing, and satellite visibility?"
  },
  {
   "actor": "Students (Groups)",
   "role_description": "Arrange the prompt cards into an argument map, drawing causal arrows between concepts and identifying the unifying principle, then test their map by explaining one concept from each domain using only the principle."
  },
  {
   "actor": "Students (Individual)",
   "role_description": "Write a final synthesis statement — three to four sentences — that answers the unifying question by tracing the periodic-motion principle from Moon phases through calendars to satellites."
  }
 ],
 "activity_title": "The Island Observatory: Designing a Sky-Based Calendar for a New Settlement",
 "activity_description": "Groups receive a brief that places them as advisers to a small coastal settlement that has just been established on a remote island. The settlers have no imported calendar. They can observe the sky and they need three things: a way to count days, a way to know when the planting season returns each year, and a way to hold a community festival on a predictable Moon phase. Each group studies a data card giving thirty days of actual sky observations — times of shortest midday shadow, Moon phase descriptions, and any visible satellite passes — and must design a working calendar system from that data alone, justify every design choice with chapter reasoning, and identify one limitation their system still carries. The act of building the calendar from raw sky data is the synthesis: every concept in the chapter is called upon in sequence as the settlers' real problem demands it.",
 "teacher_notes": "This unit's vehicle — a design brief solved from a data card — is distinct in kind from the scenario-card causal-tracing and cause-and-effect map construction used in the shorter plans' final units; it demands synthesis through construction rather than through analysis or mapping. Every concept it requires (phases, reflective illumination, day from Earth's rotation, month from phase cycle, year from season cycle, the 11-day shortfall, the intercalary correction, calendar types, satellite twilight visibility) appears across all unit-title maps given. Write the data card on the board or copy it for each group before the period; suggested values are: shadow shortest at approximately the same clock-reading each day (confirming the 24-hour day); Moon described as full on Day 1, then gibbous-waning through Day 7, half-circle waning on Day 8, crescent waning Days 12–14, no Moon visible Day 15, crescent waxing Day 16, gibbous waxing Day 22, full again Day 30 (one complete phase cycle in ~29–30 days); satellite passes noted at dusk on two evenings. These numbers are internally consistent with the chapter and require no calculation beyond simple addition. The limitation round is non-optional: it closes the design loop and prevents students from leaving with the impression that any single calendar system is perfect. If a group designing a lunar calendar says its limitation is 'it will drift from the seasons,' accept this only if they can state by how many days per year and why — push for the 11-day figure. If a group chooses a luni-solar design, their limitation should reference the complexity of deciding when to add the intercalary month. The satellite data on the card is not decoration: it anchors Section 11.4 and ensures the unit draws the whole chapter in, not only the calendar sections.",
 "materials": [
  "Data cards (one per group) — each card lists 30 days of sky observations: daily shortest-shadow time, Moon phase description for each day, and two dusk satellite-pass notes; these can be written on the board or printed on paper",
  "Blank paper for the calendar design (one sheet per group)",
  "Pencils or pens",
  "Rulers (optional, for drawing calendar grids)"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-7",
   "activity": "Teacher reads the settlement brief aloud and distributes or writes up the data card. Without explanation, teacher asks: 'Before you design anything, what does this data already tell you about the sky?' Groups spend three minutes reading the card silently and annotating what they notice — shortest-shadow regularity, Moon phase progression, satellite pass timing — then share one observation each in a rapid round. Teacher does not confirm or correct yet; the observations seed the design task."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "7-20",
   "activity": "Groups begin designing their calendar. The brief requires them to answer three questions in writing on their design sheet: (1) How will the settlers count a day, and what in the data supports that unit? (2) How will they mark a month, and how long is it according to the data? (3) How will they know the planting season is returning — and if they use only lunar months, what problem will appear within a few years? As groups work, teacher circulates and poses probing questions only when a group has stalled: 'What is the Moon doing during those thirty days — and why does it look that way from Earth?' or 'The settlers want to plant when the rains return each year — which cycle tells them that?' Groups are not required to choose the same calendar type; a group may choose lunar, solar, or luni-solar, and each choice is acceptable if justified."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "20-30",
   "activity": "Each group adds two further items to their design sheet: (4) On which Moon phase should the settlers hold their community festival, and on which day of their calendar does it fall this cycle? They must name the phase, state where the Moon would appear in the sky at sunset on that evening, and explain why it looks that way — tracing the appearance back to reflected sunlight and the geometry of Earth–Moon–Sun. (5) Two evenings in the data show a fast-moving point of light crossing the sky shortly after sunset. The settlers ask what it is. Groups must write a one-sentence explanation using the chapter's reasoning about artificial satellites, their orbital altitude, and why they are visible only near twilight."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "30-40",
   "activity": "Each group names their calendar type (lunar, solar, or luni-solar), reads their answer to question 3 aloud — the drift problem or how they solve it — and states their one honest limitation. Teacher records the limitation from each group on the board without comment until all have spoken. Class then compares: which limitations are the same across different calendar types, and which are unique to one type? Teacher asks whether any calendar design on the board has no limitation at all. The expected answer is no, and the class articulates why: because the three astronomical cycles — day, month, year — do not divide into each other evenly, every calendar must make a choice about which cycle to prioritise and accept an approximation in the others."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "40-45",
   "activity": "Teacher closes by returning to the data card and naming what each piece of data stood for: the regular shortest shadow is Earth's rotation giving the day; the Moon's thirty-day phase cycle is the Moon's revolution giving the month; the settlers would need a full year of shadow observations to pin down the solar year, but they now know the cycle exists and why. The satellite pass at dusk is the final thread: even in a remote settlement, the same orbital mechanics that govern the Moon also place human-made objects in the sky on predictable schedules. Teacher names the chapter's central idea in one sentence — the day, the month, and the year are not inventions; they are measurements of three orbital motions that were already happening — and notes that the settlers on the data card were doing exactly what every calendar-making civilisation did: reading the sky carefully enough to build time from it."
  }
 ],
 "homework": [],
 "pedagogical_approach": "Design-based learning: students apply the chapter's conceptual framework to an unfamiliar but realistic problem, constructing an artefact (the calendar design) whose internal logic must be consistent with orbital-motion reasoning. The design constraint — no imported calendar, only sky observations — forces students to re-derive the day, month, and year from first principles rather than recall definitions, and the limitation-identification task at the close ensures they hold the nuance that every calendar system involves a trade-off."
}

