 "period_number": 12,
 "period_duration_minutes": 45,
 "progression_stage": 5,
 "stage_label": "Analyse cascading disruptions and evaluate ecosystem consequences",
 "synthesis": true,
 "roles": [
  {
   "actor": "Teacher",
   "role_description": "Frames the synthesis task, provides a blank ecosystem relationship map template, and circulates to challenge students to make cross-concept connections they have not yet articulated explicitly."
  },
  {
   "actor": "Students (Individual)",
   "role_description": "Construct the relationship map integrating all chapter concepts, then use it to evaluate the disruption scenario in writing, tracing at least three linked consequences with named relationship types at each step."
  }
 ],
 "activity_title": "The Mangrove Shoreline: Reading a New Ecosystem Whole",
 "activity_description": "Students receive a profile card describing a coastal mangrove creek — a setting not used anywhere in the chapter — that includes a sketch map, a short species list with feeding notes, and three abiotic measurements taken before and after a nearby shrimp farm was built. Working in pairs, they annotate the profile to classify every relationship shown, construct a food web from the species list, identify which decomposer role is represented, and then trace the two-step cascade that follows one specific abiotic change recorded on the card. The pair's completed annotation sheet becomes the record of the chapter drawn together in a single, unfamiliar place.",
 "teacher_notes": "Print or hand-draw one profile card per pair before the lesson; the card's content is fully specified in the materials list and can be reproduced on a single A4 sheet. The species list should include: mangrove trees (producers), fiddler crabs (detritivores/omnivores), mudskippers (carnivores), herons (carnivores), mosquito larvae (consumers), mangrove fungi (decomposers), and flowering mangrove plants with visiting sunbirds (mutualism pair). The abiotic data should show three values — dissolved oxygen, silt level, and salinity — each with a 'before farm' and 'after farm' reading. Choose changes that allow a clear two-step cascade: for example, increased silt reduces sunlight penetration (abiotic–abiotic), which reduces mangrove photosynthesis (abiotic–biotic), which reduces leaf litter (biotic–biotic, affecting decomposers and crabs). The cascade the students trace is not given to them; they select one abiotic change from the data and reason forward. \n\nThe most common error is students who label an arrow 'frog eats insect' type correctly but then treat each interaction as independent; circulate in the annotation phase and ask pairs: 'If this relationship changed, which other arrow on your sheet would change next?' This cross-referencing question is the synthesis move. \n\nIn the closing band, name the chapter's core ideas explicitly — habitat, biotic and abiotic components, population, community, ecosystem, food chain, food web, trophic level, decomposer, mutualism, commensalism, parasitism, competition, cascade, ecosystem services — and show how each appeared on the card. This is earned closure because students can point to the annotation on their sheet where each idea did work.",
 "materials": [
  "One mangrove creek profile card per pair (A4, teacher-prepared): includes a labelled sketch map of the creek shoreline, a species list with brief feeding notes for each species, and a two-column abiotic data table showing dissolved oxygen, silt level, and salinity before and after a nearby shrimp farm was established",
  "Coloured pens or pencils in at least three colours (one per interaction category: biotic–biotic, biotic–abiotic, abiotic–abiotic)",
  "A legend strip (small printed or hand-written reference, one per pair) listing the three interaction categories, the five relationship types (producer/consumer/decomposer, mutualism, commensalism, parasitism, competition), and a one-line reminder of what a trophic level means — students may not have all terms equally fresh"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-7",
   "activity": "Teacher distributes the profile cards face-up and gives pairs two minutes to read silently. Teacher then asks the class: 'Without writing anything yet — what kind of place is this, and what living and non-living things does it contain?' Two or three pairs share aloud. Teacher confirms the habitat framing and states the task: 'Your job is to annotate this card so that everything it shows is classified and connected. By the end, your card should look like the whole chapter happened here.'"
  },
  {
   "minutes": "7-20",
   "activity": "Pairs annotate the species list and sketch map for interactions. Using the three colours, they draw and label arrows between organisms and between organisms and abiotic components, writing the relationship type beside each arrow (e.g., 'biotic–biotic / predation', 'mutualism', 'decomposer → soil nutrients → mangrove tree'). They also construct a food web by arranging the species in feeding order and connecting them with arrows, labelling each trophic level. Teacher circulates, prompting pairs who have labelled interactions in isolation to ask which arrow would change if the one they are looking at changed."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "20-32",
   "activity": "Pairs turn to the abiotic data table. Each pair selects one abiotic change shown in the before/after columns and traces a two-step cascade on the card: Step 1 — how that abiotic change affects one biotic component (labelling the interaction type); Step 2 — how that biotic change affects another organism or abiotic component further along. They record their cascade as two connected arrows added to the sketch map, each arrow annotated with the mechanism. Teacher checks that both arrows are mechanistically labelled, not just directional."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "32-40",
   "activity": "Whole-class share: teacher asks two pairs with different chosen abiotic changes to describe their two-step cascades aloud while the rest of the class checks their own annotation for the same chain. Class discusses whether the two cascades interact — if silt increases and oxygen drops, do the consequences compound or stay separate? Teacher draws both cascades on the board as a combined web, showing that the ecosystem's response is not a single chain but an interconnected set of responses."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "40-45",
   "activity": "Teacher closes by reading aloud the chapter's central ideas — habitat, biotic and abiotic components, population, community, ecosystem, food chain, food web, trophic level, decomposers, the five relationship types, cascade, ecosystem services — and after each one asks: 'Where did this appear on your card?' Pairs point to or tap the relevant annotation. The final statement is not a summary but an observation: the mangrove creek card required every one of these ideas to be read correctly, which is what it means for an ecosystem to work as a system."
  }
 ],
 "homework": [],
 "pedagogical_approach": "Collaborative annotation: students work in pairs to mark up a structured data card rather than producing free-form writing, making every concept they apply visible on the same sheet. The profile card is designed so that no single concept from the chapter is sufficient — every annotation depends on at least one other idea — so the synthesis is enacted through the task itself rather than stated in a summary."
}

