{
 "period_number": 12,
 "period_duration_minutes": 45,
 "progression_stage": 5,
 "stage_label": "Evaluate threats to Earth's balance and the consequences of disruption across systems",
 "synthesis": true,
 "roles": [
  {
   "actor": "Teacher",
   "role_description": "Presents the synthesis scenario, clarifies that students should draw on the full chapter, and facilitates a class discussion to surface and resolve disagreements in the cascading-effects chains."
  },
  {
   "actor": "Students (Groups)",
   "role_description": "Work through the scenario collaboratively, constructing a cascading-effects diagram that connects the planetary condition degraded to sphere disruptions, life-sustaining function losses, and reproductive continuity threats, then match each consequence to a response scale."
  }
 ],
 "activity_title": "The Pale Blue Dot Has a Problem: Diagnosing a Fictional Planet",
 "activity_description": "Students receive a profile of a fictional Earth-like planet called Verdana, described through five data cards covering its orbital position, size and gravitational pull, atmospheric composition, magnetic field strength, dominant life forms and their reproductive strategies, and one reported change event. Working in groups, they diagnose which of Verdana's life-sustaining conditions are stable, which are compromised, and how the reported change is cascading through Verdana's four sphere-equivalents to threaten reproductive continuity — then present a two-minute diagnosis to the class. The planet's conditions are drawn deliberately from every concept the chapter develops, so completing the diagnosis is itself the act of synthesising the chapter whole.",
 "teacher_notes": "Verdana is constructed so that every chapter strand is load-bearing: students cannot complete the diagnosis without drawing on the habitable zone and liquid water, gravity and atmosphere retention, the ozone shield, the magnetic field, the four spheres, photosynthesis and respiration, asexual and sexual reproduction, and the threat-cascade logic of the triple planetary crisis. Print or write one set of five data cards per group before the lesson; the card content is embedded in the opening band and can be copied onto card-sized slips of paper or the board. The reported change event on card 5 is a weakening of Verdana's magnetic field — this is chosen because it links cleanly to solar-wind damage to the atmosphere, ozone reduction, UV increase, photosynthesis disruption, reproduction harm, and food-web collapse, touching every chapter thread without privileging any single shorter plan's emphasis. Resist the temptation to confirm or correct group diagnoses during work time; save all substantive feedback for the closing band, where student reasoning — correct or partially wrong — becomes the material for consolidation. If a group finishes early, ask them to identify which of Verdana's conditions most closely resembles Mars and which most closely resembles early Earth, and to explain why using chapter evidence.",
 "materials": [
  "Five data cards per group (teacher-prepared before the lesson by copying the card content from the opening band onto paper slips or index cards — no printing equipment required)",
  "One blank 'Diagnosis Sheet' per group: a half-sheet of paper folded into four columns headed Stable Condition / Compromised Condition / Sphere Affected / Consequence for Life Continuity",
  "Pencils or pens",
  "Blackboard or whiteboard for the teacher to record converging diagnosis points during the closing band"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-8",
   "activity": "Teacher introduces Verdana: 'Astronomers have just sent back data on a planet in another solar system. It looks like it could support life — but something is going wrong. Your group is the diagnostic team. You have five data cards. Read them carefully, because everything on them matters.' Teacher distributes the five data cards (or directs groups to read them from the board). Card 1 — Orbital position: Verdana orbits its star at a distance that allows liquid water on its surface; average surface temperature 14 °C; water covers 68 % of the surface. Card 2 — Size and gravity: Verdana's mass is slightly smaller than Earth's; gravity is strong enough to hold nitrogen, oxygen, and carbon dioxide in its atmosphere, but only just; atmospheric pressure is 85 % of Earth's. Card 3 — Atmosphere: 21 % oxygen, 0.04 % carbon dioxide, trace ozone layer present; mild greenhouse effect keeps surface temperatures stable; the ozone layer is thin but functional. Card 4 — Life forms: photosynthesising plant-equivalents cover 40 % of land; animal-equivalents reproduce sexually with external fertilisation in water bodies; one dominant land species reproduces asexually through vegetative structures in dry seasons. Card 5 — Reported change event: over the past century, Verdana's magnetic field has weakened by 30 %; solar wind particle bombardment reaching the upper atmosphere has increased; scientists have recorded a 12 % reduction in ozone layer thickness and a measurable rise in surface UV radiation. Groups read all five cards silently, then the teacher asks: 'Before you begin the diagnosis, tell me — which card worries you most, and why?' Two or three quick responses from the class establish that the magnetic field weakening on card 5 is the driver, and that its effects will spread."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "8-28",
   "activity": "Groups work on their Diagnosis Sheets, filling in the four columns for Verdana. Teacher circulates and uses targeted questions rather than answers to push reasoning: 'Card 3 says the ozone layer is already thin — what does card 5's UV rise do to the plant-equivalents on card 4, and how does that connect to the animal-equivalents that reproduce in water?' 'Card 2 says gravity is only just strong enough to hold the atmosphere — what happens to atmospheric pressure if the ozone layer keeps thinning and solar wind keeps stripping gases?' 'The land species reproduces asexually in dry seasons — which chapter idea tells you whether that offspring population will have the variation to adapt to rising UV?' Groups are expected to populate all four columns: at least two stable conditions (orbital position maintaining liquid water; gravity retaining atmosphere for now), at least two compromised conditions (ozone layer; magnetic shielding), at least three spheres named as affected (atmosphere from ozone loss; hydrosphere from UV affecting water-based fertilisation; biosphere from photosynthesis disruption and food-web damage; geosphere indirectly through soil nutrient cycling breaking down as decomposers decline), and at least two consequences for life continuity (external fertilisation in water bodies disrupted by UV damage to gametes; asexual reproduction producing identical offspring with no variation to adapt to the new UV environment, threatening the land species with extinction if conditions shift further)."
  },
